                                "identity": "fnv1a64:db192b4e45aa7000",
                                "lines": "1: # F-flow-law — Stationary flow algorithm and reference cases\n2: \n3: Draft 1. Task `T-design-flow`. Input: [F-contract draft 1.1](PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md),\n4: SHA-256 `48FF446850E0DD31A0AE5E20F803923DB9F6E22A384A9532E7144E04F4CFDC07`.\n5: \n6: ## 1. Decision and scope\n7: \n8: Specify one candidate: a uniform Cartesian staggered (MAC) finite-volume layout,\n9: paired donor-cell momentum/mass transport, implicit backward-Euler Newtonian\n10: viscous stress, and a nonincremental pressure projection solved by PCG with\n11: unmodified level-zero incomplete Cholesky, IC(0). No alternative solver menu or\n12: third-party dependency is introduced. This is a design specification, not code\n13: or an adopted physical foundation. **All numerical values and acceptance limits\n14: remain PROPOSED / UNAPPROVED**, including inherited F-contract values.\n15: \n16: The stationary geometry here consists of full Cartesian cells, face-aligned\n17: walls/impermeable membranes and rectangular periodic or open boundaries.\n18: Environmental and explicitly resolved internal liquid use the same algorithm,\n19: with separate physical ownership and boundary labels. Constant reference areal\n20: density rho=1 kg/m² is used throughout the reference cases. Variable-density\n21: multiphase flow, moving cuts, curved-wall approximations, swept volumes and their\n22: coupling laws are outside this task. In particular, stair-stepping the circular\n23: chamber in F-contract is not an authorized substitute for its geometry.\n24: \n25: F-contract exists; current [Substrate source](../Substrate/Substrate.cpp) still\n26: contains pilot telemetry and false-returning checkpoint hooks, not a fluid solver.\n27: The handoff permits design without pretending the unapproved budgets are approved.\n28: Local capability guidance used: `bitloop` for ownership, and `simulation-health`\n29: for local checks, horizons and evidence. No new Bitloop execution is claimed.\n30: \n31: The staggered pressure/projection and sparse incomplete-Cholesky background was\n32: checked against [Bridson and Müller-Fischer's course notes, chapters 2 and 4](https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~rbridson/fluidsimulation/fluids_notes.pdf).\n33: We choose IC(0), rather than their later modified variant, to specify one bounded\n34: factorization without modification parameters. Projection-method background is\n35: also in [Chorin's original paper](https://math.berkeley.edu/~chorin/chorin68.pdf).\n36: Our units, conservation rules, thresholds, worked calculations and rejection\n37: decision below are project proposals/derivations; those sources do not validate\n38: this candidate or its performance.\n39: \n40: **Decisive limitation:** section 7 derives a 12.5% flow/power error for the selected\n41: no-slip stencil in a four-cell-wide channel. F-contract's 0.25 m minimum passage\n42: is four cells wide at h=1/16 m. This exceeds the proposed 2% flow/work comparison\n43: gate. The design is specified, but this combination cannot be adopted at that\n44: resolution without an explicitly reviewed change. No baseline or budget is changed\n45: by this document.\n46: \n47: ## 2. State, units and topology\n48: \n49: All physical state is C-fluid-owned within C-world's committed revision. C-boundary\n50: supplies fixed geometric/connectivity data and owns no inventory. Use F-contract's\n51: area units: A in m², rho in kg/m², p in N/m, velocity in m/s, force in N, energy\n52: in J. Kinematic viscosity nu has units m²/s; dynamic viscosity mu=rho nu has\n53: units kg/s. Viscous traction is force per length, not three-dimensional pressure.\n54: \n55: | Location | Stored or derived quantity |\n56: | --- | --- |\n57: | Cell centre c | Owned constituent amounts q[c,k] in kg. Total mass M[c]=sum_k q[c,k]. Pressure p[c] is the accepted constraint multiplier/warm-start, not a stored-energy reservoir |\n58: | Vertical face (i+1/2,j) | One x-momentum degree of freedom P_x and its normal velocity u=P_x/m_x |\n59: | Horizontal face (i,j+1/2) | One y-momentum degree of freedom P_y and velocity v=P_y/m_y |\n60: | Momentum dual volume | Derived inertial mass m_f: half the mass from each adjacent cell; at an outer boundary, the one available half-cell. It is not an additional material inventory |\n61: | Fixed geometry | Cell area h², face length h, half/full dual volumes, wall/port tags and explicit allowed adjacencies. Blocked faces have zero transport and distinct physical sides |\n62: | Derived caches | Component labels, dense pressure/momentum rows, sparse operators, IC factors and flux scratch, all tagged by geometry/coefficient revision |\n63: \n64: Each component's dual volumes partition its domain for that momentum component;\n65: summing x-dual and y-dual masses together would double-count material and is\n66: forbidden. Velocities represent a piecewise-constant dual-volume field using\n67: face-centre quadrature. Its energy is `K_h=0.5 sum_f P_f²/m_f`, summing x and y\n68: contributions. Its integrated momentum is `(sum P_x, sum P_y)`. Reference field\n69: samples and continuum integrals are reported separately; quadrature error cannot\n70: be hidden by comparing only discrete totals.\n71: \n72: Constant-density incompressibility means target M[c]=rho h². Do not reset an\n73: advected inventory to that target. Reconstruct dual inertia from the actual\n74: owned masses; reject if `abs(M[c]/(rho h²)-1)>1e-8`, or if F-contract's tighter\n75: mass/volume budgets fail. The permitted discrepancy is a monitored numerical\n76: residual, not a compressible phase model. All reference initializations set\n77: M[c]=rho h² exactly; species merely partition that mass. Binary64 arithmetic\n78: and fixed row/reduction order define the reference execution configuration.\n79: \n80: Domain/port/material IDs are stable references. Cell indices and solver rows\n81: are dense implementation indices tied to a grid revision. A membrane disconnects\n82: both primal transport and the momentum/viscosity stencils; no interpolation,\n83: preconditioner edge or ghost value may bridge its two sides. Corner contact\n84: alone does not connect pressure regions. Flood-fill only permitted open faces.\n85: \n86: An explicitly resolved internal domain has its own grid inventories and momentum;\n87: the associated chamber must not also own well-mixed contents for the same material.\n88: Automatic conversion between those representations is excluded. A future physical\n89: opening creates an explicit allowed adjacency/transfer; mere spatial proximity\n90: does not merge internal and environmental fields.\n91: \n92: ## 3. Conservative transport and one staged substep\n93: \n94: Use interval-start divergence-free velocity u^n for all explicit transport over\n95: Delta t. First-order time integration is intentional and must be measured under\n96: refinement. Final projected u^(n+1) drives the next interval, not a retroactive\n97: second inventory update in this one. The trace must distinguish transport fluxes\n98: actually used from endpoint velocities.\n99: \n100: For oriented primal face a→b, `Q_f=ell_f u_f^n` in m²/s. Upwind density and\n101: composition come from the donor cell (a for Q≥0, b otherwise):\n102: `Phi_f=Q_f M_d/A_d`, `Phi_f,k=Phi_f q_d,k/M_d`, in kg/s. The same signed\n103: `Delta t Phi_f,k` is subtracted from a and added to b, once. Reservoirs carry\n104: explicit external postings. No limiting by independent recipient clamping and\n105: no negative-amount clipping; an invalid proposal rejects.\n106: \n107: Construct momentum-dual mass fluxes by the arithmetic half-sums of adjoining\n108: primal mass fluxes. For an x-face dual volume, its left/right fluxes are half\n109: the two bracketing x-face mass fluxes; top/bottom fluxes are half the y-fluxes of\n110: the two adjoining half-cells. Interchange x/y for y momentum. At a physical\n111: boundary use only its actual half-cell, with prescribed boundary flux; never\n112: average across a blocked face. Opposite postings use one stored dual flux.\n113: This construction makes dual mass change exactly the corresponding half-sum\n114: of primal mass changes, up to measured rounding error.\n115: \n116: Advect the represented component momentum using that dual mass flux times the\n117: donor dual velocity component. Reconstruct `m_f^(a)` from updated cell inventories\n118: and set `u_f^(a)=P_f^(a)/m_f^(a)`. Thus mass and momentum use the same transported\n119: material; no separate interpolation of velocity bypasses conservation. This\n120: donor-cell transport has numerical diffusion. Its kinetic-energy residual is\n121: measured separately from viscosity and is never available fuel/heat. Constituent\n122: diffusion and reactions are zero here; detailed species laws remain downstream.\n123: \n124: Substep order, entirely in candidate buffers:\n125: \n126: 1. Validate geometry, connected components, density/inventories and interval-start\n127:    velocity divergence. Select Delta t under section 6 and freeze all boundary data.\n128: 2. Form primal/dual fluxes and stage inventories and momenta conservatively.\n129: 3. Add prescribed body-force impulses `Delta P=m_f^(a) g Delta t`. Meter force\n130:    work with the corresponding average of pre/post-force velocities.\n131: 4. Apply the coupled implicit viscous solve from section 4, obtaining u*.\n132: 5. Apply section 5's pressure projection, obtaining endpoint velocity and pressure.\n133: 6. Recompute true residuals, all local/global accounts and validity checks. Commit\n134:    together, or discard the entire attempted substep and use C-world's bounded\n135:    retry/prefix semantics. Every stage's allocation/solve/rejected work counts.\n136: \n137: Prescribed velocity faces are constraints, not unconstrained momenta silently\n138: overwritten after the solve. Account their removed momentum equation as support/\n139: inlet reaction and work, including viscous and pressure contributions. Static\n140: solid walls do zero work but can exchange impulse and angular impulse.\n141: \n142: ## 4. Viscous stress and work\n143: \n144: Use a single coupled backward-Euler solve for Newtonian symmetric stress\n145: `tau=mu(grad u + grad u^T)`, with constant nonnegative mu. On the MAC grid take\n146: normal derivatives at cell centres and shear `d_y u + d_x v` at corners, using\n147: centred differences and the actual half-distance to a face-aligned wall.\n148: The discrete dissipation quadratic form is the area-weighted sum\n149: `2 mu[(d_x u)²+(d_y v)²] + mu(d_y u+d_x v)²`.\n150: Assemble its symmetric nonnegative stiffness K_v by the transpose of the same\n151: strain operator used to compute stresses. This includes cross-component terms;\n152: do not substitute unrelated scalar filters when constructing wall reactions.\n153: \n154: For free momentum DOFs, with M_d=diag(m_f^(a)), solve\n155: `(M_d + Delta t K_v) u* = M_d u^(b) + Delta t f_wall`.\n156: Here u^(b) follows the body-force step and f_wall comes from eliminated prescribed\n157: boundary values. Use PCG/IC(0) as for pressure, with a 200-iteration cap and true\n158: relative L2 residual ≤1e-8 or nondimensional absolute L2 residual ≤1e-10.\n159: K_v has rigid modes, but M_d makes the viscous system SPD; no pressure-style\n160: gauge pin is applied to it. Nonpositive mass, invalid pivots or nonconvergence\n161: reject the attempt. For mu=0 this stage is exactly the identity.\n162: \n163: For homogeneous stationary walls/periodicity and an exact solve,\n164: `Delta K_visc = -Delta t (u*)^T K_v u* - 0.5 ||u*-u^(b)||²_Md`.\n165: The first term is physical discrete viscous dissipation; the second is\n166: backward-Euler numerical damping. With nonzero wall data, add actual wall work\n167: and evaluate strain using those data. Record the residual of this identity from\n168: the true algebraic solve. Keep the energy loss from transport, viscous time\n169: integration, pressure projection and physical stress in separate columns.\n170: \n171: For the channel below, tangential u is at y_j=(j+1/2)h. A stationary no-slip wall\n172: uses zero wall velocity at distance h/2: ghost u_-1=-u_0. Consequently the first\n173: row of the scalar shear reduction is `(u_1-3u_0)/h²`; interior rows are\n174: `(u_(j+1)-2u_j+u_(j-1))/h²`. This is the chosen stencil, including its quantified\n175: coarse-passage error. Do not silently replace it with a different wall formula.\n176: \n177: ## 5. Pressure, boundaries and disconnected components\n178: \n179: ### 5.1 Compatible pressure operator\n180: \n181: Let B be integrated divergence on free normal-velocity faces: for a face oriented\n182: from cell a to b its column is +ell in a and -ell in b. Pressure-open exterior\n183: faces have the one signed entry for their incident cell. Known velocity boundary\n184: fluxes form q_N, positive outwards. Therefore continuity is `B u + q_N=0`.\n185: This integrated operator must be the transpose partner of the pressure force;\n186: do not independently code incompatible divergence and gradient stencils.\n187: \n188: Let b_p have signed `ell p_boundary` on pressure-open faces, and zero internally.\n189: For the full physical pressure, not a pressure increment, solve\n190: \n191: `A_p p = b,    A_p = B M_d^-1 B^T`\n192: \n193: `b = -(B u* + q_N)/Delta t + B M_d^-1 b_p`\n194: \n195: `u^(n+1) = u* + Delta t M_d^-1 (B^T p - b_p)`.\n196: \n197: B u has units m²/s; A_p p and b have units m²/s². On a full constant-density\n198: interior square face, the matrix edge weight is `ell²/m_f=1/rho`; dividing a\n199: row by cell area recovers the usual 1/(rho h²) coefficient. A pressure boundary\n200: has half a momentum dual volume, giving weight 2/rho (distance h/2), not an\n",
                                "next_offset": 200,
                                "path": "docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md",
