                                "identity": "fnv1a64:577e838481f954a5",
                                "lines": "1: # F-contract — Shared physical and state contract\n2: \n3: Revision: draft 1.1, 2026-10-09. MindGraph task: `T-contract`.\n4: \n5: ## 1. Status, inputs and scope\n6: \n7: This is a specification for independent numerical designs, not an implementation,\n8: solver selection, calibrated material model, or qualification result. The T-loop\n9: human approval supplied with this task authorizes continuation after the pilot;\n10: it does **not** approve the numerical values proposed here.\n11: \n12: **Every numerical fixture parameter, tolerance, horizon and resource limit in\n13: sections 5–8 is PROPOSED / UNAPPROVED.** Freeze an identified revision by human\n14: decision before using it to accept a numerical candidate. A failed candidate\n15: cannot change that revision's budgets to make itself pass. Mandatory ownership,\n16: paired accounting and honest failure follow D-accounting and D-failure.\n17: \n18: Inspected inputs: [project instructions](../AGENTS.md),\n19: [vision](../VISION.md), [pilot specification and findings](DIAGNOSTIC_PILOT.md),\n20: the actual [scene source](../Substrate/Substrate.cpp) and\n21: [header](../Substrate/Substrate.h), and retained pilot `checks.json` and\n22: `observations.json` in `.codex/runs/run0003_20261009_130412/`.\n23: The scene currently has lifecycle counters only; checkpoint hooks return false.\n24: The selected task in `.mindgraph/codex.local.json` lists T-loop as its dependency\n25: and no required input artifacts. Approval provenance is the user's T-loop handoff,\n26: not an inference from files. There is no existing physical core to preserve.\n27: \n28: A-scope remains the coupled proof followed by the interactive laboratory,\n29: including crawling, swimming and pumping. This contract first specifies passive\n30: chamber qualification and only reserves active interfaces. The 256-organism\n31: world, damage/repair campaign, thermal state and ten-minute endurance campaign\n32: are later work. No dependencies, framework changes or general extension system\n33: are introduced here.\n34: \n35: ## 2. Authoritative ownership and identity\n36: \n37: | Owner | Authoritative state/responsibility | Reads or derives; never owns again |\n38: | --- | --- | --- |\n39: | C-world | Committed world revision, actual time, stable-ID allocation, material definitions, advancement schedule, validity policy, committed exchange totals | Coordinates the component stores below as one world; does not duplicate their arrays |\n40: | C-mechanics | Structural connectivity, rest geometry, constitutive parameters, positions and mechanical degrees of freedom, structural mass and momentum, support constraints | Receives content/fluid reactions; does not own their mass or inventories |\n41: | C-contents | Chamber membership, constituent amounts, total content mass, reference area/compressibility parameters and content inertia closure | Actual enclosed area and moments come from authoritative structural geometry; pressure and stored compression energy derive from that area and inventory |\n42: | C-fluid | Each resolved control volume's mass/species and consistent momentum representation; accepted fluid state | Face fluxes, pressure corrections, cut geometry and reconstruction caches are derived unless required for reproducible continuation |\n43: | C-boundary | No physical inventory; derives side-labelled cuts, wall kinematics, swept regions, transfer routes, traction/work mappings and support transitions | Reads structures; supplies proposals to the actual owners. A temporary flux record is not stored material |\n44: | C-biology | Construction requests, deterministic controller/reaction state, local sensing rules and resource-limited activity requests | Reads a constrained committed-state view; fuel remains in its owning inventory |\n45: | C-app | Scenario configuration, Bitloop adapter, observation/recording and explicit serialization | Calls world advancement; rendering, trace formatting, UI and file output never enter physical kernels |\n46: \n47: Use typed stable IDs for organisms, structural vertices/elements, chambers,\n48: material regions, supports and transfer ports where they have persistent physical\n49: meaning. IDs are unique within a world; an ID/generation pair is never silently\n50: reused. Restore the allocator and generations. Dense slots may move on compaction;\n51: external requests contain IDs and a world revision, not slots or pointers.\n52: Transient iteration indices, solver rows, quadrature points and cut fragments do\n53: not need permanent public IDs. Identify diagnostic grid locations by grid revision,\n54: cell coordinate and physical side. Connectivity through a cell is not evidence\n55: that opposite sides of a membrane communicate.\n56: \n57: One material amount has exactly one owner. Chamber interiors contain no resolved\n58: fluid inventory in these fixtures. The exterior grid covers only the exterior;\n59: there is no ghost copy of the chamber mass in its cells. Structural material has\n60: its own mass. The zero-thickness membrane below excludes no additional fluid area.\n61: Inventory-to-mass conversion belongs to material definitions; a cached total mass\n62: is a derived sum, never an additional conserved amount.\n63: \n64: ## 3. Units, interfaces and exchange signs\n65: \n66: ### 3.1 Area-based two-dimensional mechanics\n67: \n68: Use metre, second and kilogram as L, T and M; represented volume means area in\n69: square metres. There is no implicit one-metre thickness and no conversion to\n70: three-dimensional pressure. Any later thickness convention needs an explicit\n71: contract revision applied to all coefficients and accounts together.\n72: \n73: | Quantity | Unit / definition |\n74: | --- | --- |\n75: | Area A, areal density rho | m²; kg/m²; mass M = rho A |\n76: | Species amount q and concentration | kg of that constituent; q/A in kg/m². Species partition total mass, not extra mass |\n77: | Velocity, momentum, angular momentum | m/s; kg m/s; kg m²/s |\n78: | Force and line tension | kg m/s² (N) |\n79: | Two-dimensional pressure/stress p | N/m = kg/s²; pressure force on length dl is p n dl |\n80: | Line density, extensional coefficient S | kg/m; N; edge tension S times engineering strain |\n81: | Work/energy, power | N m (J); J/s; pressure work p dA |\n82: | Fuel-specific energy | J/kg, explicitly declared before active use |\n83: \n84: Geometry is right-handed x/y; positive rotation is counterclockwise. Chamber\n85: loops are counterclockwise; n points from chamber to exterior. Gauge pressure\n86: zero is the exterior reservoir. Uniform gauge shifts change no net closed-loop\n87: force, but boundary work must use one consistent pressure reference.\n88: \n89: ### 3.2 Minimal data crossing ownership boundaries\n90: \n91: These are semantic records, not mandated C++ classes or an asynchronous bus.\n92: Use ordinary project-owned functions/data and stable references; no logging SDK\n93: or parallel world abstraction is required.\n94: \n95: | Operation | Inputs | Output and obligations |\n96: | --- | --- | --- |\n97: | Advance interval | Committed revision/time, positive requested duration, external schedule, work limits | Accepted duration, actual committed time/revision, completion or failure reason, attempt/work counts and bounded offending IDs |\n98: | Mechanical proposal | State view, duration, loads/constraints/material law | Candidate geometry/velocity, strain/energy, support reactions; no visible commit |\n99: | Boundary derivation | Old/proposed structures and duration | Oriented facets, side connectivity, exact polygon area/moments, swept-area mapping and minimum-support observations |\n100: | Content response | Chamber ID, candidate actual area/moments, candidate owned amounts and motion | Pressure, compression energy, inertia loads and finite proposed exchanges; no independent area evolution |\n101: | Fluid proposal | Owned grid state, sided old/new control volumes, wall motion, outer boundary data | Candidate inventories/momentum, pressure traction, integrated fluxes, convergence residuals and work |\n102: | Paired exchange | Revision, interval, source/receiver IDs, route/side, signed amounts/impulses/work | One record consumed exactly once by both owners at commit; invalid or stale references reject the proposal |\n103: | Observation | Committed revision and permitted local region/ports | Read-only quantities with units, sample time and validity; no access to candidate buffers |\n104: \n105: Orient an inventory route a→b: positive `Delta q` removes that constituent from\n106: a and adds it to b. Its carried momentum and energy use the **same** transferred\n107: amount and time integration, with equal/opposite postings. Transferred constituent\n108: masses sum to total transferred mass; for a nonzero transfer their dimensionless\n109: mass fractions sum to one. No negative donor inventory, teleportation or\n110: unpaired transfer is accepted. Exterior reservoir routes have an explicit external\n111: ledger rather than a fictional finite owner inside the simulation.\n112: \n113: Orient a mechanical exchange a→b: `J` is impulse delivered to b; a receives\n114: `-J`. Record application points or equivalent couples so angular momentum also\n115: closes. `W` is work delivered to b using the same contact/traction kinematics;\n116: a receives `-W`, apart from separately identified physical storage/dissipation.\n117: Internal pressure uses these same facet motions: chamber expansion dA>0 delivers\n118: `+p_ch dA` to the wall, and content compression energy changes by `-p_ch dA`.\n119: Exterior pressure force on the wall is `-p_ext n dl`. With equal constant pressure\n120: on both sides, the net pressure load is zero. Stationary supports do zero work;\n121: moving supports supply measured reaction work even when their net impulse is zero.\n122: \n123: For each owner, account for advected energy and mechanical work exactly once;\n124: for the whole domain use\n125: `r = Delta(K + U) - W_external - E_advected_in + E_advected_out + D_physical`.\n126: Here U includes actual elastic/compression stores, D_physical≥0 is declared\n127: physical dissipation, and r is numerical residual. Keep signed sums, sums of\n128: absolute local residuals and sums of positive residuals. Numerical loss is not\n129: heat, fuel or an energy reservoir. Open boundaries require both pressure work\n130: and advected kinetic/stored-energy flux; logging pressure alone is insufficient.\n131: \n132: ### 3.3 Content inertia and reserved active interfaces\n133: \n134: Well-mixed composition does not mean zero content mass or zero inertia. Require\n135: a stated velocity closure over the chamber with density M/A. It must reproduce\n136: rigid translation/rotation and affine dilation exactly: momentum M v_c, rotational\n137: inertia `(M/A) integral |x-c|² dA`, and kinetic energy\n138: `0.5 (M/A) integral |v(x)|² dA`. Inertial reactions, including geometry-dependent\n139: terms, must be derived consistently from that closure. A boundary mass copy is\n140: not permitted. Non-affine closure/interpolation is a numerical-design choice:\n141: each candidate must state it, demonstrate positive kinetic energy and paired work,\n142: and freeze it before the non-affine tests. Matching those integral requirements\n143: is the interface requirement; this document does not select an interpolation.\n144: \n145: Reserve only these active operations, inactive in every passive fixture:\n146: \n147: - Finite transfer request: connected port IDs, donor/receiver side, constituent\n148:   amounts or rates integrated over a duration, and available-donor limits. Return\n149:   actual accepted amounts and paired momentum/energy. Permeability laws deferred.\n150: - Fuel-limited stress request: actuator/material IDs, bounded target stress/strain,\n151:   duration, named fuel owner and a declared work-cost/efficiency law. Reserve fuel\n152:   before staging, settle actual cost on commit, release reservations on rejection.\n153:   Zero available fuel cannot supply positive active work; numerical residual never\n154:   replenishes fuel. Detailed metabolism and constitutive actuation laws deferred.\n155: - Constrained sensing request: allowed attached elements/ports or finite spatial\n156:   radius, named observables, sampling interval and committed revision. Controllers\n157:   cannot read global solver settings, hidden distant fields or trial states.\n158:   Controller state/clock and any RNG are transactional. Neural/evolution algorithms\n159:   are deferred; these records do not prescribe a general controller framework.\n160: \n161: ## 4. State lifecycle, failure and restart\n162: \n163: Initialize and validate state at t=0 before any process tick. Initial observation\n164: is distinct from post-commit tick 1. For requested interval [t,t+H], C-world stages\n165: candidate substeps against the last valid committed state. Exchanges, geometry,\n166: solves, inventories and safety checks succeed together before a substep commits.\n167: Readers see only a committed revision. Drawing or the number of viewports cannot\n168: advance time; the app must not use the pilot's frame counter as physical time.\n169: \n170: Choose **prefix commit** semantics: accepted substeps may remain committed when\n171: a later substep cannot advance. Return `advanced < requested` and the actual time;\n172: the failed substep leaves the preceding committed state bitwise unchanged,\n173: including inventories, counters, RNG, controller clocks and physical ledgers.\n174: Attempt statistics may change in an external diagnostic record. The app reports\n175: the incomplete interval and stops that experiment; it cannot label t+H as reached\n176: or silently continue frozen dynamics. A later explicit retry starts from that\n177: reported prefix, not from a fabricated full-frame state. Full interval rollback\n178: is not required and must not be presumed by consumers.\n179: \n180: Validate finite state, legal topology/IDs, positive areas/masses, nonnegative\n181: species, minimum support, paired exchanges, pressure convergence and quality\n182: budgets before commit. Invalid candidate state is never made visible. Bound retries\n183: and iteration work; exhausting any cap returns a reason (invalid configuration,\n184: unsupported geometry, solver nonconvergence, invalid candidate or work limit),\n185: first failing time and affected IDs. Never clip negative amounts, remove thin\n186: walls, mix disconnected fragments, skip elapsed time or freeze active terms to pass.\n187: Restoration/staging may use journals, reusable buffers or copies; measure all costs.\n188: \n189: Checkpoint-relevant state: schema/configuration/material/contract identities;\n190: committed time, tick/substep schedule and revision; ID allocation/generations;\n191: connectivity/rest state; owned geometry/velocities/momenta and mass/species;\n192: chamber reference-area/energy parameters and inertia closure state; fluid state;\n193: controller/RNG state and future external-event cursor; cumulative physical and\n194: numerical accounts needed to continue the same experiment. Save caches or warm\n195: starts if their omission changes the accepted trajectory/iteration path; otherwise\n196: declare a deterministic rebuild. Do not serialize uncommitted proposals as restart\n197: state. Validate/load into a candidate world, then swap on success; failed load\n198: preserves the live world. View/camera state is separate. Future save/restore work\n199: must compare uninterrupted continuation; the existing false-returning hooks supply\n200: no such guarantee today.\n",
                                "next_offset": 200,
                                "path": "docs/PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md",
                                "total_lines": 515
                            }
