                                "lines": "281: limit: propose `nu Delta t/h²≤0.25` and use dt refinement to measure its extra\n282: damping. For a sudden body force also require `|g| Delta t²/h≤0.25`; this is a\n283: screen, not a substitute for endpoint CFL and pressure checks. No acoustic CFL\n284: is claimed for this incompressible model.\n285: \n286: Follow F-contract's four halvings, at most 512 substep attempts per output,\n287: and prefix-commit failure semantics. A stationary split has one pressure solve\n288: per attempt, not four automatic coupling passes. IC breakdown, nonconvergence,\n289: unbalanced components, stale topology, density drift or exceeded residual/energy\n290: budgets all fail explicitly. Repeated solves cannot consume unlimited work.\n291: Previous accepted state/time, inventories, pressure warm start and physical\n292: accounts survive rejection. Diagnostic attempt counters may advance separately.\n293: \n294: Face-aligned passages preserve every open face; never close a narrow channel to\n295: improve conditioning. Geometry below F-contract's fixed g_min=0.25 m is unsupported.\n296: Meeting g_min is necessary but does not establish accuracy: the selected viscous\n297: wall stencil needs more than four cells for the proposed channel gate (section 7).\n298: Do not merge regions or discard cells based on a condition-number estimate.\n299: Fractional cut faces/tiny cut cells are not supported by this full-cell law;\n300: their inertia, flux, stress and pressure operators require the separate boundary\n301: design, retaining these transpose and ownership identities.\n302: \n303: ## 7. Worked references and local/work oracles\n304: \n305: These values are calculations, not observed simulation results. All run gates\n306: below are proposals requiring approval. Use inherited mass, impulse, leakage,\n307: pressure and energy accounts without weakening them. Additional viscous cases\n308: have declared physical dissipation and are distinct from F-contract's nu=0 cases.\n309: \n310: ### R0 — Uniform pressure/rest and uniform momentum\n311: \n312: Use a 1×1 m box, h=1/16 m, rho=1, M_total=1 kg, zero velocity. For the open\n313: rest case set all reservoir pressures to p0=3 N/m and compatible zero-flow\n314: incoming state. Every cell has M=1/256 kg, every gradient and Q is zero, and the\n315: matrix equation is satisfied by p=p0 because B^T p=b_p. Pressure work, viscous\n316: dissipation and energy change are all zero. Require the F-contract Q0 local\n317: speed/pressure/energy limits; verify pressure on every cell, not just mean flow.\n318: \n319: Repeat with closed and periodic boundaries: p0 and p0+7 N/m must produce the\n320: same flow; the solver's pinned pressure is zero, so compare gradients after\n321: gauge alignment rather than failing because it did not retain an arbitrary p0.\n322: Repeat as two disconnected boxes with separate pins and perturb only one RHS;\n323: the other component's velocities/inventories must not change.\n324: \n325: Uniform-momentum variant: 2×1 m periodic box, u=0.2 m/s, v=-0.1 m/s, rho=1,\n326: any constant mu≥0. Every dual transport divergence and every strain is zero.\n327: Expected P=(0.4,-0.2) kg m/s and K=0.05 J, unchanged. Require local velocity\n328: error ≤1e-10 m/s, component momentum closure under F-contract and per-output\n329: energy residual ≤1e-12 J. This exercises nonzero advection without confusing\n330: numerical diffusion of gradients with conservation of uniform motion.\n331: \n332: ### R1 — No-slip channel and power\n333: \n334: Domain length L=2 m, width Hc=1 m, periodic x and stationary no-slip y walls.\n335: rho=1 kg/m², nu=0.1 m²/s, mu=0.1 kg/s, constant acceleration g_x=0.1 m/s².\n336: G=rho g_x=0.1 N/m² represents a metered mean pressure gradient; do not also\n337: apply a pressure drop on the periodic seam. Base h=1/16 m, Delta t=0.00125 s.\n338: \n339: Continuum steady solution:\n340: `u(y)=G y(Hc-y)/(2 mu)`, v=0,\n341: `u_max=G Hc²/(8 mu)=0.125 m/s`,\n342: `Q=G Hc³/(12 mu)=0.0833333333333 m²/s`,\n343: `P_body=G L Q=0.0166666666667 W`.\n344: The two wall reactions sum to `-G L Hc=-0.2 N`; each wall's shear magnitude is\n345: G Hc/2=0.05 N/m. Wall work is zero. Physical dissipation\n346: `mu L integral (du/dy)² dy` equals P_body, and\n347: `K= rho L G² Hc^5/(240 mu²)=0.00833333333333 J`.\n348: \n349: For the specified half-distance stencil, the exact discrete steady values are\n350: `u_j=G[y_j(Hc-y_j)+h²/4]/(2 mu)`, y_j=(j+1/2)h.\n351: The pointwise offset from the sampled continuum is `G h²/(8 mu)`;\n352: relative to the exact dual-strip averaged velocity it is `G h²/(6 mu)`.\n353: The measured discrete flux is\n354: `Q_h=sum_j h u_j=Q+G Hc h²/(6 mu)`, so relative flux/power error is\n355: `2(h/Hc)²`. This includes quadrature error; reporting only centre samples would\n356: understate the integrated flow error. Discrete wall shear remains exactly G Hc/2.\n357: \n358: | Ny | h (m) | Pointwise offset (m/s) | Q_h (m²/s) | Flow/power error | Discrete power (W) |\n359: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n360: | 16 | 0.0625 | 0.00048828125 | 0.083984375 | 0.78125% | 0.016796875 |\n361: | 32 | 0.03125 | 0.0001220703125 | 0.08349609375 | 0.1953125% | 0.01669921875 |\n362: | 64 | 0.015625 | 0.000030517578125 | 0.0833740234375 | 0.048828125% | 0.0166748046875 |\n363: \n364: A future implementation must match the discrete solution to ≤1e-8 m/s at every\n365: sample and the continuum pointwise error to ≤0.5% u_max; require flux and work\n366: error ≤2% and wall-force error ≤2%. Compare local face traction and the positive\n367: viscous quadratic sum to the body-force power. Its steady dissipation should\n368: equal **discrete** power; additionally report the difference from continuum\n369: power as spatial discretization error, not a physical source.\n370: \n371: For the force-then-viscosity split, metered force work over a step also includes\n372: the average-velocity quadrature term `0.5 M_total g_x² Delta t²`. In exact steady\n373: discrete flow this is cancelled by the viscous stage's BE numerical damping;\n374: the physical power values above use the steady velocity. Retain both terms in\n375: the stage audit rather than misclassifying the extra term as physical dissipation.\n376: \n377: Initialize once with the exact discrete steady solution to isolate wiring, and\n378: separately from rest to exercise viscosity. The slowest continuum startup decay\n379: time is `Hc²/(pi² nu)=1.013211836 s`; use 10 s then 20 s, retaining startup cost\n380: and errors. The continuum startup reference is\n381: `u(y,t)=sum_(n odd) [4 g_x Hc²/(nu pi³ n³)] [1-exp(-nu(n pi/Hc)²t)] sin(n pi y/Hc)`.\n382: Bound its series remainder below one tenth of the comparison tolerance. A\n383: direct steady-only run does not establish startup behavior or solve convergence.\n384: \n385: ### R2 — Viscous decay with conserved mean momentum\n386: \n387: Use the 2×1 m periodic box, rho=1, nu=0.1, v=0 and\n388: `u(y,0)=U0+a0 sin(2 pi y/Hc)`, U0=0.2 m/s, a0=0.1 m/s, Hc=1 m.\n389: Advection is identically zero for this shear field: no x dependence and v=0.\n390: Continuum amplitude `a(t)=a0 exp(-nu k²t)`, k=2 pi/Hc, time constant\n391: tau=0.2533029591 s. Mean momentum P_x=0.4 kg m/s is constant;\n392: `K(t)=0.5 rho L Hc U0² + rho L Hc a(t)²/4`.\n393: Initial K=0.045 J, mean-motion K=0.04 J. At t=1 s,\n394: a=0.0019296302911 m/s and K=0.0400018617365 J.\n395: Physical dissipation rate is `mu L Hc k² a(t)²/2`, and its time integral equals\n396: lost fluctuation energy. It cannot consume the uniform mean motion.\n397: \n398: Sample at the MAC face centres. The discrete eigenvalue is\n399: `lambda_h=4 sin²(pi/Ny)/h²`, and backward Euler predicts\n400: `a_h(n)=a0 [1+nu lambda_h Delta t]^(-n)`.\n401: Check each face against that discrete field and compare amplitude, momentum and\n402: continuum energy separately. The missing within-dual-cell variation is a spatial\n403: quadrature approximation, not deleted inventory. If a reference initializes\n404: exact dual averages instead of samples, multiply both initial reference and\n405: discrete amplitudes by sinc(pi/Ny); never mix those initialization conventions.\n406: \n407: | Ny | Delta t (s) | a_h at 1 s (m/s) | Error against continuum (m/s) |\n408: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n409: | 16 | 0.00125 | 0.00204882191633 | 0.00011919162522 |\n410: | 32 | 0.000625 | 0.00196369311403 | 0.00003406282293 |\n411: | 64 | 0.0003125 | 0.00194046492771 | 0.00001083463661 |\n412: \n413: For each viscous step, with amplitude changing a_old→a_new, discrete physical\n414: dissipation is `Delta t nu lambda_h (rho L Hc/2) a_new²` and numerical damping\n415: is `(rho L Hc/4)(a_new-a_old)²`. Their sum must equal the lost discrete kinetic\n416: energy to the algebraic-solve tolerance. Do not label their sum physical viscosity.\n417: Pressure should add no loss to this already divergence-free mode.\n418: \n419: Propose local error against the discrete eigenmode ≤1e-8 m/s, absolute amplitude\n420: error against continuum ≤0.5% a0, momentum error ≤1e-10 kg m/s, and physical\n421: work/energy comparison error ≤2% of initial fluctuation energy 0.005 J.\n422: Apply F-contract's separate cumulative numerical-residual budget with E*=0.005 J,\n423: not the larger mean-motion energy. Inspect 0, 0.25, 0.5 and 1 s, then extend to\n424: 2 s (>7 tau) to expose residual mean damping; tiny late amplitudes use the fixed\n425: initial-amplitude scale, never a vanishing denominator. Require dt-only and\n426: h/dt refinement as well as the shown combined sequence.\n427: \n428: ### R3 — Narrow passages, isolation and rejection controls\n429: \n430: Repeat R1 with physical Hc=0.25 m, L=2 m and the same G/mu. At base h=1/16 m,\n431: Ny=4. Analytic Q=0.00130208333333 m²/s, whereas the selected discrete law gives\n432: Q_h=0.00146484375 m²/s: **12.5% high**, and body power is 12.5% high as well.\n433: At 8 and 16 cells across the same physical gap the errors are 3.125% and 0.78125%.\n434: Pointwise error divided by the true peak velocity is 1/Ny²: 6.25%, 1.5625% and\n435: 0.390625%, respectively. The 2% flow/work gate needs at least 10 cells across;\n436: the local 0.5%-of-peak-velocity gate needs at least 15. The power-of-two\n437: refinement satisfying both uses h=1/64 m, 16 cells. Geometry support alone\n438: is therefore insufficient at the proposed base grid. This is an analytic rejection\n439: of this stencil/resolution for that gate, not a measured runtime result and not\n440: authorization to refine the whole coupled experiment or weaken the budget.\n441: \n442: Also define two 1×1 m chambers separated by a face-aligned sealed membrane, and\n443: two 1×1 m chambers joined by a length-1 m, width-0.25 m straight corridor on the\n444: same h grid. For the sealed case require two pressure components and zero\n445: cross-wall scalar/momentum/viscous stencil entries. For the open corridor require\n446: one component, finite conductance and paired through-flow; compare section/face\n447: fluxes and entrance pressure fields against a separately refined run. Fully\n448: developed Poiseuille values apply inside a straight periodic channel, not at a\n449: chamber entrance. Do not mislabel entrance losses as a failed Poiseuille oracle.\n450: \n451: Invalid controls: an all-velocity sealed component with net prescribed inflow,\n452: opposite imposed flux imbalances in disconnected components that cancel globally,\n453: nonpositive IC pivots, an artificially restricted iteration cap, and a corridor\n454: below 0.25 m. Each must fail before commit with actual time/state preserved.\n455: No islands may disappear because they are small; no single global gauge or global\n456: RHS correction may conceal a component's incompatible flux.\n457: \n458: ## 8. Iteration, memory and full-cost instrumentation\n459: \n460: Report per component and per solve: active cells/unknowns, boundary types, gauge\n461: cell, compatibility correction, nonzeros, initial/final true residuals, maximum\n462: cell divergence/volume defect and its identity, IC minimum pivot, PCG iterations,\n463: matvecs, triangular solves, rebuilds, breakdown reason and accepted/rejected time.\n464: Include prescribed-pressure work, support reactions, momentum/constituent closure,\n465: transport dissipation, physical viscosity, BE damping and projection loss.\n466: Aggregate both signed and absolute local errors; a zero total is insufficient.\n467: \n468: Use compressed sparse rows with binary64 coefficients, checked 32-bit column IDs\n469: and 64-bit row offsets; reject index overflow. For a 5-point full-cell pressure\n470: matrix, a planning estimate is 5N entries for A, 3N for lower IC and eight N-long\n471: binary64 working arrays: about `60N+36N+16N+64N=176N` bytes, or 2.75 MiB for\n472: N=16384, plus row-end constants. This is **not** total simulation memory or a\n473: measured allocation. Add geometry/labels/indices, inventories, momentum arrays,\n474: both candidate and committed state, face masses/fluxes, viscosity matrix/factors,\n475: boundary data, journal/retry storage, thread scratch and allocator capacity.\n476: Count shared buffers once but concurrent peaks honestly. Record actual allocated\n477: bytes, high-water marks and process working-set/private-byte peaks separately.\n478: \n479: Time the complete requested H including transport, viscosity/pressure assembly,\n480: factor construction, both solves, safety checks, work accounting, restoration and\n",
