                                "lines": "201: \n202: ## 5. Proposed common fixture definition\n203: \n204: All values below require approval. They are synthetic scales, not measured tissue.\n205: All physics quantities/accumulators use binary64 for the reference campaign; report\n206: any different precision as a separate candidate. No randomness; fixed construction\n207: order and seed recorded as “unused”. Gravity, viscosity, membrane damping,\n208: permeability, reactions and active stress are zero in the base inviscid fixture.\n209: Any physical damping extension is a separately identified fixture with an energy law.\n210: \n211: ### 5.1 Geometry and constitutive reference\n212: \n213: - Domain: disk of radius Ro=4 m, centred at (0,0), embedded in an 8×8 m Cartesian\n214:   grid. Only its interior is active. Exterior fluid occupies the region outside\n215:   the chamber and inside the disk, density rho_f=1 kg/m², initially at rest.\n216:   The disk boundary is a pressure reservoir p=0 with compatible incoming state\n217:   (same constituent/density, zero tangential velocity); log signed mass, momentum\n218:   and energy fluxes. Do not also impose zero normal flux. This permits expansion\n219:   of a chamber in incompressible exterior fluid without an impossible closed-box\n220:   volume constraint. Internal walls are impermeable and free-slip.\n221: - Chamber: regular counterclockwise N=128 polygon of circumradius R0=1 m,\n222:   vertices `(cos(2 pi i/N), sin(2 pi i/N))`. All coordinates and exact polygon\n223:   area/moments, not pi R², define the discrete authoritative geometry.\n224:   `A0 = N R0² sin(2 pi/N)/2`; `l0 = 2 R0 sin(pi/N)`.\n225: - Membrane: material line density lambda=0.1 kg/m, fixed structural mass\n226:   `M_s = lambda N l0`, half each edge mass to its two endpoints. Rest lengths l0;\n227:   edge strain e=(l-l0)/l0; tension T=S e with S=1 N; energy per edge\n228:   `S (l-l0)²/(2 l0)`. No bending or compression buckling law is claimed. Base\n229:   compliant tests stay tensile or at rest; imposed compression uses a kinematic\n230:   wall variant explicitly replacing the elastic response, rather than accepting\n231:   an unsupported compressed membrane model.\n232: - Contents: one constituent, rho_ref=1 kg/m², owned mass M, `Aref=M/rho_ref`,\n233:   K=10 N/m. `p_ch = K(Aref/A - 1)` and\n234:   `U_ch = K[A - Aref - Aref ln(A/Aref)]`, so `-dU_ch/dA = p_ch` at fixed mass.\n235:   This synthetic barotropic law and affine-consistent inertia define the passive\n236:   content contract. Use A/Aref in [0.8,1.2]; outside it is unsupported, not clipped.\n237:   Transferred-mass energetics of this law are deferred with active transfer laws.\n238: - Base equilibrium: M=rho_ref A0, zero velocities, p_ch=p_ext=0, zero strain.\n239:   Dynamic expansion variant: M=1.10 rho_ref A0, initial A=A0 (p_ch=1 N/m),\n240:   zero velocities. This changes initial inventory, not an unaccounted mid-run source.\n241: - Grid h=1/16 m (128×128 bounding grid); temporal base substep dt=0.00125 s,\n242:   calculated output interval H=0.02 s. Compliant material spacing l0≈0.0491 m.\n243:   Refinements double N and halve h; reuse the same continuum parameters, not\n244:   the same per-edge mass or spring constant.\n245: \n246: Minimum supported free-fluid passage width g_min=4 h_base=0.25 m, held fixed in\n247: physical units on finer grids. Minimum separation of opposed surfaces is also\n248: 0.25 m for this campaign. This excludes adjacent elements and pairs on the same\n249: smooth membrane neighbourhood within 0.5 m of rest arclength; otherwise even the\n250: initial polygon's second-neighbour vertices would spuriously violate the limit.\n251: Membranes have zero geometric thickness but retain\n252: explicit side topology at every resolution. Require positive edge lengths ≥0.5 l0\n253: and finite simple non-self-intersecting chamber boundaries. Grid alignment can\n254: produce arbitrarily small cut fragments despite these physical feature limits;\n255: this is not grounds to delete material or relax g_min. Exact zero-measure cuts\n256: may change representation only through an accounted support transition.\n257: \n258: ### 5.2 Required cases and independent oracles\n259: \n260: Each case includes per-owner/local checks as well as global totals. A common\n261: global area or energy sum cannot cancel a local sign/side error. Reset state for\n262: each case; forces generated by prescribed wall motion are external and metered.\n263: \n264: | ID | Construction and forcing | Local oracle | Global/reference oracle |\n265: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n266: | Q0 equilibrium | Base chamber; also repeat at offsets (0,0), (h/2,h/2), (h/3,h/5) and rotations 0, pi/7 | Every velocity, edge strain and pressure difference stays zero within budgets; equal/opposite traction on each facet | No centre drift, net force/torque, area change or unforced energy gain |\n267: | Q1 free expansion | Compliant inflated-inventory chamber, released from rest in exterior fluid | Area-derived pressure, tensile strain, radial velocity field and facet work; record nonradial modes instead of averaging them away | Circular continuum reference below plus polygon refinement; radius extrema, phase, momentum and accounted energy agree |\n268: | Q2 compression/recovery | Prescribed regular polygon radius `R(t)=1-0.05[1-cos(2 pi t/2)]` m; M=rho_ref A0. Period 2 s; R spans 0.9–1. Replace membrane elastic loads by the moving support, retain structure/content inertia | Exact polygon A(t)=A0 R(t)²/R0², p(A), swept volume and local normal velocity; no wall leakage | Over each cycle chamber U returns to initial; integrated support work, open-boundary energy flux and residual close. Compression work on contents is positive |\n269: | Q3 sealed translation | Prescribed base polygon centre `c_x=0.5 sin²(pi t/2)` m, c_y=0, period 2 s; orientation fixed; starts at rest | Zero relative wall-normal flux; constant content amounts/area; adjacent fluid and wall motion remain compatible | Net fluid volume constant, content translation inertia M v_c, paired total impulse/support work. Drag/flow values compared under refinement, not asserted zero |\n270: | Q4 sealed rotation | First prescribed base polygon, then equal-area ellipse sampled with semiaxes a=1.25 m, b=0.8 m; `theta=(pi/2)[1-cos(2 pi t/2)]`, period 2 s | Exact rigid polygon area, rigid content angular inertia from area moments; side preservation at rotating corners | Circular continuum/free-slip limit has no exterior flow from purely tangential wall motion; finite polygon departures must converge. Ellipse pressure torque/work is not assumed zero; compare refinements |\n271: | Q5 narrowing/support | Add two impermeable capsule-shaped supports and the prestressed chamber defined below, initially at rest. `g(t)=0.375+0.125 cos(2 pi t/2)` m, period 2 s; minimum 0.25 m. Translate supports in y and account their external work | Fluid can flow around ends; monitor both gaps, fragment areas, connectivity, swept-area fluxes, pressure extrema and neighbouring side inventories | No artificial force/work/inventory jump when facets cross grid faces/vertices; compare integrated reactions and flow fields under grid/phase refinement. Keep chamber compliant; actual deformed clearance, not nominal g, determines support validity |\n272: | Q6 bounded failure | Q5 supports with `g(t)=0.5-0.3 sin²(pi t/4)` m for 0≤t≤2 s, chamber shape fixed; separately force pressure-solve failure on a chosen attempt and supply invalid nonfinite input | Reject unsupported g<0.25 before commit; rejected candidate changes no committed physical state, RNG or accounts | Actual time/prefix and reason match the last successful substep; retry/iteration limits hold; no fabricated success, disappearing wall or silent frozen motion |\n273: \n274: Q5's supports are externally prescribed rigid solids: all points within distance\n275: r_cap=0.125 m of the horizontal segment from (-2,y_c) to (2,y_c), with\n276: `y_c=±(1+g+r_cap)`. Their nearest surfaces are at y=±(1+g), giving the stated\n277: nominal gaps. Each solid excludes area `8 r_cap + pi r_cap²` from the fluid.\n278: Their geometry belongs to the mechanical supports; exterior actuators absorb\n279: reaction impulse/work, with no finite support inventory inside the world.\n280: Use the same capsule geometry for collision, fluid cuts and swept-area mapping.\n281: If unsupported, report that result; do not substitute a different fixture.\n282: \n283: Q5 starts in a tensile equilibrium rather than an unstressed ring subject to\n284: immediate external compression. Keep the R0 polygon, set rest edge length\n285: `l_rest=l0/1.1`, S=1 N and structural mass `lambda N l_rest`; the initial tension\n286: is T0=0.1 N. Set content mass `M=rho_ref A0[1+T0/(K R0 cos(pi/N))]` so uniform\n287: internal pressure exactly balances the discrete radial edge forces. Fluid starts\n288: at p=0 and v=0. Edge strain uses l_rest in this case. Monitor tensile validity\n289: throughout; if any edge requires negative tension or the actual gap drops below\n290: the supported limit, the compliant case is unsupported and must be reviewed,\n291: not repaired by clipping tension. The kinematic Q6 variant uses the base polygon\n292: and inventory and does not claim this compliant response.\n293: \n294: For Q3/Q4 the driven wall replaces compliance only to isolate sealed-motion\n295: coupling; it does not qualify deformable mechanics. Q1 and Q5 do. For Q5 add\n296: translations of the entire fixture relative to the grid and phases selected so a\n297: facet passes within 1e-2, 1e-4 and 1e-6 h of a grid vertex, on both sides. Exact\n298: alignment is a separate case. Record the actual minimum cut fraction and cell IDs.\n299: Apply the same physical geometry to refinement runs; do not scale the gap with h.\n300: \n301: For Q1 use an independent circular, radial reference (not the candidate's own\n302: pressure solver). Let R be radius, v=dR/dt, a=d²R/dt²,\n303: `A=pi R²`, `Aref=1.1 pi R0²`, `M=1.1 rho_ref pi R0²`,\n304: `M_s=2 pi R0 lambda`. Exterior radial velocity is `u(r)=R v/r`. With p(Ro)=0,\n305: the wall-side exterior pressure is\n306: `p_f(R)=rho_f[(R a+v²) ln(Ro/R) + 0.5 v²(R²/Ro²-1)]`.\n307: Integrate the scalar equation\n308: `(M_s+M/2) a = 2 pi R[p_ch-p_f(R)] - 2 pi S(R/R0-1)`.\n309: Its acceleration coefficient after collecting terms is positive:\n310: `M_s + M/2 + 2 pi rho_f R² ln(Ro/R)` for R<Ro.\n311: The M/2 term follows from uniform content affine-dilation kinetic energy M v²/4.\n312: Include kinetic energy carried out of the open outer boundary in its energy check.\n313: Converge the independent reference to ≤1/10 of the candidate comparison budgets\n314: before use. Finite-polygon area, mass and force differences are discretization\n315: errors to measure under N refinement, not errors to erase by substituting pi R².\n316: \n317: ## 6. Proposed budgets and refinement gate\n318: \n319: Define scales once per fixture before running: L*=R0=1 m, A*=A0, U*=1 m/s,\n320: M*=rho_ref A0, P*=K=10 N/m, J*=M* U*, and a fixed nonzero energy scale E*.\n321: For Q1 E* is its initial compression energy (about 0.152 J); for Q2–Q5 E* is\n322: the larger of 0.01 J and the peak kinetic energy, change in stored energy from\n323: its initial value, or absolute work per cycle\n324: from the independently converged reference run. Freeze that value before testing\n325: candidates. Never use a candidate's growing energy or a large background store\n326: as its own denominator. For Q0 use the explicit absolute tolerances below.\n327: \n328: Let interval residual r_k cover one H=0.02 s output interval, including every\n329: accepted substep/exchange. Local residuals are owner/cell/facet terms assigned\n330: using one declared partition of storage and work. Report their signed sum and\n331: L1 sum; count an internal exchange once in the combined L1 audit. Values cannot\n332: cancel by aggregating unrelated sides. Cumulative budgets do not reset per cycle.\n333: \n334: | Quantity | Proposed local / interval limit | Proposed whole-horizon / reference limit and rationale |\n335: | --- | --- | --- |\n336: | Identity, finite state, side topology | Zero invalid IDs, nonfinite values, negative inventory, self intersections or forbidden routes; failed candidates reject | Zero committed hard violations; these are validity requirements, not average error budgets |\n337: | Mass/species balances | Paired postings use one amount; residual per owner per H ≤1e-10 M* | Absolute signed global closure ≤1e-8 M* after external fluxes; also sum absolute owner residuals ≤1e-7 M*. Floating-point accounting allowance, not permitted physical loss |\n338: | Impulse / torque pairing | Pair residual ≤1e-10 J* and 1e-10 J* L* per H | Global residual after external impulses ≤1e-7 J* and 1e-7 J* L*. Advected momentum included |\n339: | Geometric area consistency | Polygon area vs sum sided cut areas: ≤1e-8 A* globally and ≤1e-7 h² per affected cell; each fragment relative error ≤1e-5 against independent clipping when its area is nonzero | Cumulative absolute swept-area/flux mismatch ≤1e-6 A*. Small fragments cannot hide behind an h² denominator alone |\n340: | Content / chamber volume | Prescribed Q2–Q4 area error ≤1e-6 A*; content pressure must use that same area | Q1/Q5 area trajectory error vs reference ≤0.5% A*, peak ≤1%; no secular volume drift over later windows |\n341: | Leakage across sealed barriers | Constituent flux across an intact wall is identically zero by route topology; reconstructed relative normal-flux residual per facet ≤1e-8 U* times facet length | Integrated absolute diagnostic leakage ≤1e-7 A* over horizon; signed cancellation does not pass. Any actual forbidden constituent transfer rejects regardless of its size |\n342: | Equilibrium / passive drift | Q0 max speed ≤1e-7 U*, max pressure imbalance ≤1e-7 P*, per-vertex displacement ≤1e-6 L* | Q0 energy gain ≤1e-10 M* U*², COM drift ≤1e-6 L*. No sustained unforced propulsion |\n343: | Energy error (Q1–Q5) | Sum absolute local residuals per H ≤0.1% E*; every local term also ≤that cap | Sum absolute residuals ≤2% E* and sum positive residuals ≤0.5% E* over full horizon. Report signed drift too; physical dissipation separately, zero in these inviscid fixtures |\n344: | Deformation / motion | Q1 nonradial displacement RMS ≤0.2% L*, max ≤0.5% L*; per-edge strain error vs mapped reference ≤0.01 absolute | Reference trajectory position RMS ≤0.5% L*, max ≤1%; radius extrema ≤1%, period ≤1%, phase error ≤0.02 cycle by horizon. Late-window error growth remains within these same limits |\n345: | Fluid field / reactions | Away from wall (distance ≥2h), Q1 radial-velocity RMS error ≤2% U*, max ≤5% U*; report near-wall errors separately using sided samples | Maximum pointwise force/torque error and per-cycle support-work error ≤2% of fixed reference peak force/torque/absolute cycle work, with zero-reference floors 1e-6 P* L*, 1e-6 P* L*², 1e-6 E*. Time-integrated force/torque use the separate impulse/angular-impulse budgets |\n346: | Pressure solve / incompressibility | Independently recomputed residual relative L2 ≤1e-8 (or absolute ≤1e-10 after fixed nondimensionalization); additionally each cell volume defect per substep ≤1e-8 of its actual fluid area | Max local divergence defect ≤1e-6 s^-1, including small fragments. Log true residuals, not preconditioned norms alone; failed solves reject |\n347: \n348: The percentage budgets protect small useful deformations/work while screening\n349: obviously dissipative or energy-producing coupling. The tighter inventory and\n350: geometry limits protect ownership and leakage. These are starting proposals, not\n351: empirically affordable tolerances. A pressure null space has a declared gauge;\n352: nondimensionalize lengths by L*, time by L*/U* and pressure by rho_f U*² before\n353: computing residual norms. Flux/GCL defects use the actual moving control volume\n354: and wall velocity, not divergence of velocity on an obsolete geometry.\n355: \n356: Refinement matrix: base (h,N,dt), temporal (h,N,dt/2) and (h,N,dt/4), spatial\n357: (h/2,2N,dt/2) and (h/4,4N,dt/4). Compare equal physical times, geometry,\n358: constitutive values and input phase. For each trajectory/field observable, define\n359: the refinement floor as 0.01 times its absolute acceptance limit from the table,\n360: using its frozen scales and the same norm; for example, the position-RMS floor\n",
                                "next_offset": 360,
                                "path": "docs/PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md",
                                "total_lines": 515
                            }
                        }
                    ],
