                "role": "review_project_plan"
            },
            "preview": [],
            "previous_reviews": [],
            "question": "Worker's concrete plan concern: The existing Q4 ellipse at its 2 s period implies an irrotational interior speed of 2.58363 m/s, giving speed/c=0.817 versus this reduction's proposed 0.1 validity screen. A suitable resolved interior or explicitly approved scale change is needed before qualification; the fixture was preserved.",
            "raw_response": "{\n  \"base_revision\": 4,\n  \"outcome\": \"keep_plan\",\n  \"reason\": \"The inspected compartment specification and algebra checker identify a genuine Q4 validity mismatch, not failed design-task work: speed/c is 0.817 versus the proposed 0.1 screen, and the acoustic crossing-time screen also fails. Declaring such limits is part of T-design-contents. Existing T-design-boundary and T-reconcile must resolve interior representation, rotation-state assumptions and fixture compatibility before T-approve authorizes implementation. A resolved interior is not automatically sufficient; its physical suitability must be established. No changed scales, relaxed limits or solver adoption are approved here. Reported algebra checks are not runtime qualification.\",\n  \"evidence_ids\": [\n    \"evidence-277379dd-e758-42ad-b086-c46987bea607\"\n  ],\n  \"changes\": [],\n  \"task_actions\": [],\n  \"follow_up\": null,\n  \"recovery\": null,\n  \"accepted_results\": []\n}",
            "receipt": {
                "auth": "chatgpt",
                "inspection": {
                    "calls": [
                        {
                            "arguments": {
                                "action": "read",
                                "limit": 200,
                                "offset": 0,
                                "path": "docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md",
                                "text": ""
                            },
                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:4fc99e02a004a262",
                                "lines": "1: # F-contents-law: pressure-bearing compartments\n2: \n3: Draft 1, 2026-10-10. MindGraph task **T-design-contents**.\n4: This is a candidate specification, not an implemented or adopted physical model.\n5: All additional numerical limits and example parameters below are **PROPOSED /\n6: UNAPPROVED**. The inherited fixture values and budgets remain unapproved too.\n7: \n8: Input inspected: [F-contract, draft 1.1](PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md), SHA-256\n9: `48FF446850E0DD31A0AE5E20F803923DB9F6E22A384A9532E7144E04F4CFDC07`.\n10: The current [scene](../Substrate/Substrate.cpp) contains only the diagnostic pilot;\n11: it has no compartment implementation and its checkpoint hooks return false.\n12: This deliverable adds no runtime dependency, solver, thermal model or alternative\n13: world. The passive proof and subsequent active laboratory remain the work-package\n14: scope; this design does not claim rich-world scale or endurance.\n15: \n16: ## 1. Meaning and ownership\n17: \n18: A compartment contains one spatially uniform composition and areal density. Its\n19: mass is real; its geometry and velocity field determine its inertia. Uniform\n20: composition is a constitutive approximation, not permission to create circulation,\n21: erase a jet, pool connected chambers or attach their mass to membrane vertices.\n22: \n23: | Owner | Authoritative state or output |\n24: | --- | --- |\n25: | C-mechanics | Oriented structural loops, vertex positions and velocities, structure mass, supports. Content inertia enters its coupled equations but is never added again as structural mass |\n26: | C-boundary | Actual area, centroid, area moments, oriented facets and their velocities, physical port locations/sides. Derived geometry owns no inventory |\n27: | C-contents | Constituent masses, material law, admissible circulation state, closure identity, and content energy/momentum accounts. Pressure, density, total mass and inertia are derived |\n28: | C-fluid | Any explicitly resolved interior instead of this compartment, and the exterior. A region cannot have both owners |\n29: | C-world | Candidate/commit boundary, stable IDs, time, exchange settlement and bounded failure |\n30: \n31: Use chamber/material/port IDs and generations with the committed revision. Dense\n32: quadrature, triangulation and solver indices are disposable. Each constituent\n33: mass q_s is in kg; M=sum(q_s)>0 and Y_s=q_s/M. Species partition M. This draft\n34: allows inert tracer constituents with the **same** barotropic material law; it\n35: does not infer mixture pressure from fuel, temperature or a gas-law particle count.\n36: Changing material parameters or reacting species requires an explicit energy law.\n37: \n38: Lengths are m, actual area A is m², rho=M/A is kg/m², pressure is N/m, inertia\n39: is kg m², circulation is m²/s, energy is J. There is no hidden thickness. The\n40: normal n points from contents to the exterior on the CCW loop. Positive wall work\n41: is delivered **by contents to wall**. Exterior loads are posted separately.\n42: \n43: ## 2. Pressure, storage and finite changes\n44: \n45: Retain the contract's single barotrope, with rho0=1 kg/m² and B=10 N/m in its\n46: fixtures (B is the contract's bulk coefficient K). For an actual area A and the\n47: current mass M, define:\n48: \n49: ```text\n50: Aref = M/rho0                    z = A/Aref\n51: pbar = B (1/z - 1)              rho = M/A\n52: U(A,M) = B [A - Aref - Aref ln(z)]\n53: e = U/M                         h = (B/rho0) ln(rho/rho0)\n54: partial U/partial A = -pbar      partial U/partial M = h = e + pbar/rho\n55: c² = dpbar/drho = B/rho0        dU = -pbar dA + h dM\n56: ```\n57: \n58: U is recoverable barotropic compression energy relative to the common p=0\n59: reference, not a thermal or metabolic reservoir. U>=0, with its minimum at\n60: A=Aref. Its fixed-area mass curvature is B/(rho0 M)>0. Negative h or pbar relative\n61: to this reference is possible and is not a negative inventory or a numerical\n62: failure. Material tension/cavitation outside the declared range is not modelled.\n63: Require z in [0.8,1.2], finite positive A and M; do not clamp pressure or invent a\n64: minimum mass to avoid a failure. Emptying a chamber is a topology/representation\n65: event requiring another design, not a valid state of this law.\n66: \n67: Area is recomputed from the actual proposed polygon, never integrated as a second\n68: independent volume. Aref changes with mass; keeping it at its initialization value\n69: after transfer would break both pressure and energy. Use stable log evaluation\n70: near z=1 to avoid cancellation in U.\n71: \n72: For a finite fixed-mass area change, the pressure used for total work is the\n73: secant `p_d = -[U(A1,M)-U(A0,M)]/(A1-A0)`, using the derivative limit at zero\n74: change. If A and M change together, choose the straight positive-state path\n75: `(A(s),M(s))`, s in [0,1], and its integrated derivatives p_d and h_d. Then\n76: `Delta U = -p_d Delta A + h_d Delta M`. Either evaluate the integrals to the\n77: accounting tolerance or split the stages with explicit intermediate energies;\n78: do not mix endpoint pressure work with an unrelated mass-energy update.\n79: \n80: For vertex coordinates x, the mean-pressure generalized wall force is\n81: `pbar grad_x A`. The finite counterpart uses a discrete area gradient satisfying\n82: `sum_i grad_d A_i dot Delta x_i = Delta A`. A straight vertex path integrated\n83: gradient supplies this identity for polygon area. Thus the posted mean-pressure\n84: work is +p_d Delta A at the wall and -p_d Delta A at contents. It is zero on a\n85: rigid translation or rotation. The inertial traction below supplies the missing\n86: loads when content momentum changes; a constant pbar alone has zero closed-loop\n87: resultant and cannot accelerate the contents.\n88: \n89: ## 3. A sealed, uniform-density inertia closure\n90: \n91: ### 3.1 Velocity is derived from normal motion and circulation\n92: \n93: Select a simply connected, fully filled, inviscid, slip-wall closure with spatially\n94: uniform vorticity. It supports non-affine boundaries through an auxiliary\n95: geometry solve; it is not a claim that an arbitrary deforming chamber has an\n96: affine velocity field. No free surface, internal obstacle or separate vortex is\n97: represented. For a sealed chamber let c be its area centroid, r=x-c, J r=(-r_y,r_x),\n98: and Gamma its independently initialized circulation. Set:\n99: \n100: ```text\n101: d = A_dot/A                  omega = Gamma/(2 A)\n102: v = c_dot + omega J r + grad(phi)\n103: Laplacian(phi) = d           in the actual chamber\n104: normal_derivative(phi) = (v_wall - c_dot - omega J r) dot n\n105: integral(phi dA) = 0         (fixes the scalar nullspace)\n106: Gamma_dot = 0               for the sealed inviscid closure\n107: ```\n108: \n109: The compatibility condition is `integral(boundary data dl)=A_dot=d A`.\n110: An inconsistent wall flux is a rejection, not a mean RHS to subtract silently.\n111: The same geometry must supply A_dot and the facet velocities. The area-average\n112: of v is c_dot by the transport identity for the centroid; the potential's mean\n113: gradient is therefore zero. Initial Gamma is specified by the experiment, usually\n114: zero. An angular wall speed is **not** an initialization or update for Gamma.\n115: \n116: This is a kinetic closure over the existing content owner, not a second set of\n117: fluid inventories. Define its exact moment and energy outputs:\n118: \n119: ```text\n120: Q = (1/A) integral(r r^T dA)       I = M trace(Q)\n121: P = rho integral(v dA)            L_c = rho integral(r cross v dA)\n122: Kc = (rho/2) integral(|v|² dA)\n123: ```\n124: \n125: Kc is positive for nonzero velocity. Rigid velocity `v=V+Omega J r` gives\n126: P=M V, L_c=I Omega and Kc=M|V|²/2+I Omega²/2. It is recovered when the initial\n127: circulation is 2 Omega A and the normal boundary motion is compatible. Affine\n128: dilation gives v=V+alpha r and Kc=M|V|²/2+M alpha² trace(Q)/2. A general affine\n129: velocity is recovered by its symmetric quadratic potential and matching uniform\n130: vorticity. These are polynomial reproduction requirements, not an instruction to\n131: force that state when the boundary only prescribes a normal velocity.\n132: \n133: In particular a circular wall spinning tangentially around contents initially at\n134: rest supplies no normal motion and no inviscid torque: Gamma remains zero, v=0.\n135: Well-mixed chemistry must not change that answer. An ellipse rotating from rest\n136: generates an irrotational interior flow, not rigid co-rotation (section 6).\n137: \n138: ### 3.2 Inertial traction and work\n139: \n140: Evaluate `a = partial_t v + (v dot grad)v` on the same candidate trajectory.\n141: The uniform-vorticity evolution above makes curl(a)=0 in the continuum closure.\n142: Recover an inertial pressure pi from:\n143: \n144: ```text\n145: grad(pi) = -rho a             integral(pi dA) = 0\n146: p_wall = pbar + pi\n147: force on wall facet = integral((pbar + pi) n dl)\n148: ```\n149: \n150: Conservative uniform body acceleration, if introduced, replaces a by a-b and\n151: adds its external work; the present passive fixtures have none. pi is a constraint\n152: reaction enforcing the reduced density/velocity assumption. It is **not** a second\n153: EOS, stored energy, or a claim that spatial density changes are resolved. Its\n154: zero-area-mean gauge keeps the compression store and the inertial reaction distinct.\n155: Calling pbar the complete local wall pressure in an accelerating chamber is wrong.\n156: \n157: For the sealed closure, continuity and zero mean pi give these identities:\n158: \n159: ```text\n160: P_dot = -integral_boundary((pbar+pi) n dl)\n161: d/dt angular momentum about fixed origin = -integral_boundary(x cross (pbar+pi)n dl)\n162: Kc_dot = -integral_boundary(pi v_wall dot n dl)\n163: U_dot = -pbar A_dot\n164: (Kc+U)_dot = -integral_boundary((pbar+pi) v_wall dot n dl)\n165: ```\n166: \n167: Post the opposite impulse and identical signed work to the wall, using the same\n168: quadrature and candidate velocities. Neither setting Kc from a wall snapshot\n169: without an inertia force, nor applying `M times acceleration` while omitting\n170: shape-dependent terms, satisfies these identities. A solver must retain the full\n171: geometry/time derivative of the closure and its coupling to wall acceleration.\n172: Do not apply a second lumped-mass reaction in addition to pi.\n173: \n174: ### 3.3 Numerical realization and limits on its cost\n175: \n176: For a reference realization select continuous quadratic potential functions on a\n177: deterministic conforming triangulation of the actual polygon. Integrate polynomial\n178: moments exactly to their required degree. Solve the mean-constrained weak form\n179: `integral grad(phi) dot grad(psi) = integral_boundary g psi - integral d psi`.\n180: It reproduces the affine examples without inventing interior mass nodes. Use\n181: matrix-free or sparse assembled projected CG with diagonal scaling, measured true\n182: residuals and the contract's bounded iteration policy. This is a proposed reference\n183: discretization, not a dependency request or an implementation in this task.\n184: \n185: Recover pi by the corresponding weak gradient equation. Recompute the full vector\n186: defect `rho a + grad(pi)` and normal-velocity defect independently; a least-squares\n187: pressure projection cannot conceal incompatible acceleration/curl. Enforce the\n188: contract's pressure/flux and work budgets, refining this auxiliary mesh separately\n189: from the exterior grid. Report quadrature, mesh and time errors independently.\n190: Quadratic C0 potentials have discontinuous element gradients: evaluate acceleration\n191: and traction in a consistent weak form including element-interface terms, not by\n192: treating a broken gradient as globally smooth. The discrete kinetic-energy\n193: derivative and posted wall work must still agree to the contract's budgets.\n194: Use a fixed maximum 200 iterations per solve and 4 coupling passes per attempt,\n195: inherited as unapproved caps; failure cannot launch an unlimited fallback solver.\n196: Degenerate triangles or loss of simple connected geometry reject the attempt.\n197: \n198: All auxiliary assembly, mesh maintenance, geometry derivatives, solves, staging,\n199: checks and retries belong to complete-work timing and memory. Report unknowns,\n200: iterations, residuals, retained matrices, mesh and scratch alongside exterior\n",
                                "next_offset": 200,
                                "path": "docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md",
                                "total_lines": 547
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "arguments": {
                                "action": "read",
                                "limit": 200,
                                "offset": 0,
                                "path": "tooling/check_contents_law.ps1",
                                "text": ""
                            },
                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:5be7dc36630a3b57",
                                "lines": "1: param([switch]$Document)\n2: $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'\n3: Set-StrictMode -Version Latest\n4: \n5: # Independent binary64 algebra checks; this does not run a Substrate simulation.\n6: function Assert-Near([string]$Name, [double]$Actual, [double]$Expected, [double]$Tolerance) {\n7:     $errorValue = [Math]::Abs($Actual - $Expected)\n8:     if (-not [double]::IsFinite($Actual) -or $errorValue -gt $Tolerance) {\n9:         throw \"$Name error $errorValue exceeds $Tolerance (actual $Actual, expected $Expected)\"\n10:     }\n11:     [pscustomobject]@{ check = $Name; value = $Actual; expected = $Expected; error = $errorValue; tolerance = $Tolerance }\n12: }\n13: function Store([double]$Area, [double]$Mass) {\n14:     10.0 * ($Area - $Mass - $Mass * [Math]::Log($Area / $Mass))\n15: }\n16: function Pressure([double]$Area, [double]$Mass) { 10.0 * ($Mass / $Area - 1.0) }\n17: function Enthalpy([double]$Area, [double]$Mass) { 10.0 * [Math]::Log($Mass / $Area) }\n18: function Head-Donor([double]$Transfer) { ((Store 1 1.1) - (Store 1 (1.1 - $Transfer))) / $Transfer }\n19: function Head-Receiver([double]$Transfer) { ((Store 1 (0.9 + $Transfer)) - (Store 1 0.9)) / $Transfer }\n20: \n21: $checks = @()\n22: $area = 128 * [Math]::Sin(2 * [Math]::PI / 128) / 2\n23: $meanR2 = (2 + [Math]::Cos(2 * [Math]::PI / 128)) / 6\n24: $inertia = $area * $meanR2\n25: $epsilon = 1e-5\n26: $checks += Assert-Near 'p = -partial U/partial A, N/m' (-((Store ($area + $epsilon) (1.1 * $area)) - (Store ($area - $epsilon) (1.1 * $area))) / (2 * $epsilon)) (Pressure $area (1.1 * $area)) 1e-8\n27: $checks += Assert-Near 'h = partial U/partial M, J/kg' (((Store $area (1.1 * $area + $epsilon)) - (Store $area (1.1 * $area - $epsilon))) / (2 * $epsilon)) (Enthalpy $area (1.1 * $area)) 1e-8\n28: $checks += Assert-Near 'h = e + p/rho, J/kg' ((Store $area (1.1 * $area)) / (1.1 * $area) + (Pressure $area (1.1 * $area)) / 1.1) (Enthalpy $area (1.1 * $area)) 1e-12\n29: \n30: # Independent edge quadrature of a regular polygon. Three-point Gauss is exact\n31: # for the cubic pressure-work and angular-impulse polynomials used here.\n32: $gaussX = @(-[Math]::Sqrt(3.0 / 5), 0.0, [Math]::Sqrt(3.0 / 5))\n33: $gaussW = @((5.0 / 9), (8.0 / 9), (5.0 / 9))\n34: $sumFx = 0.0; $sumFy = 0.0; $sumPower = 0.0; $sumTorque = 0.0\n35: $spinPower = 0.0; $spinTorque = 0.0; $dilatePower = 0.0\n36: $alpha = 0.1; $beta = 0.2\n37: for ($edge = 0; $edge -lt 128; ++$edge) {\n38:     $theta0 = 2 * [Math]::PI * $edge / 128\n39:     $theta1 = 2 * [Math]::PI * ($edge + 1) / 128\n40:     $x0 = [Math]::Cos($theta0); $y0 = [Math]::Sin($theta0)\n41:     $dx = [Math]::Cos($theta1) - $x0; $dy = [Math]::Sin($theta1) - $y0\n42:     for ($point = 0; $point -lt 3; ++$point) {\n43:         $s = ($gaussX[$point] + 1) / 2\n44:         $x = $x0 + $s * $dx; $y = $y0 + $s * $dy\n45:         $weight = $gaussW[$point] / 2\n46:         $piTranslate = -(0.03 * $x + 0.04 * $y)\n47:         $fx = $piTranslate * $dy * $weight\n48:         $fy = -$piTranslate * $dx * $weight\n49:         $sumFx += $fx; $sumFy += $fy\n50:         $sumPower += $fx * 0.2 + $fy * (-0.1)\n51:         $sumTorque += $x * $fy - $y * $fx\n52:         $piSpin = 0.5 * 0.1 * 0.1 * ($x * $x + $y * $y - $meanR2)\n53:         $spinTorque += $piSpin * (-$x * $dx - $y * $dy) * $weight\n54:         $spinPower += $piSpin * ((-0.1 * $y) * $dy - (0.1 * $x) * $dx) * $weight\n55:         $piDilation = -0.5 * $beta * ($x * $x + $y * $y - $meanR2)\n56:         $dilatePower += $piDilation * $alpha * ($x * $dy - $y * $dx) * $weight\n57:     }\n58: }\n59: $checks += Assert-Near 'translation wall reaction Fx, N' $sumFx (-$area * 0.03) 1e-12\n60: $checks += Assert-Near 'translation wall reaction Fy, N' $sumFy (-$area * 0.04) 1e-12\n61: $checks += Assert-Near 'translation wall power, W' $sumPower (-$area * (0.2 * 0.03 - 0.1 * 0.04)) 1e-12\n62: $checks += Assert-Near 'translation torque, N m' $sumTorque 0 1e-12\n63: $checks += Assert-Near 'initialized rigid spin torque, N m' $spinTorque 0 1e-12\n64: $checks += Assert-Near 'initialized rigid spin wall power, W' $spinPower 0 1e-12\n65: $checks += Assert-Near 'affine dilation inertia power, W' $dilatePower (-$area * $meanR2 * $alpha * $beta) 1e-12\n66: \n67: # Continuum Q1 point from the contract, including the exterior kinetic flux.\n68: $radius = 1.02; $speed = 0.1; $outer = 4.0\n69: $mass = 1.1 * [Math]::PI; $shell = 0.2 * [Math]::PI\n70: $logRadius = [Math]::Log($outer / $radius)\n71: $p = Pressure ([Math]::PI * $radius * $radius) $mass\n72: $acceleration = (2 * [Math]::PI * $radius * ($p - $speed * $speed * ($logRadius + 0.5 * ($radius * $radius / ($outer * $outer) - 1))) - 2 * [Math]::PI * ($radius - 1)) / ($shell + $mass / 2 + 2 * [Math]::PI * $radius * $radius * $logRadius)\n73: $contentKdot = $mass * $speed * $acceleration / 2\n74: $contentUdot = -$p * 2 * [Math]::PI * $radius * $speed\n75: $rho = $mass / ([Math]::PI * $radius * $radius)\n76: $piWall = -$rho * $radius * $acceleration / 4\n77: $wallPower = ($p + $piWall) * 2 * [Math]::PI * $radius * $speed\n78: $checks += Assert-Near 'free expansion content energy plus wall work, W' ($contentKdot + $contentUdot + $wallPower) 0 1e-12\n79: $exteriorKdot = [Math]::PI * ((2 * $radius * $speed * $speed * $speed + 2 * $radius * $radius * $speed * $acceleration) * $logRadius - $radius * $speed * $speed * $speed)\n80: $shellKdot = $shell * $speed * $acceleration\n81: $shellUdot = 2 * [Math]::PI * ($radius - 1) * $speed\n82: $outerFlux = [Math]::PI * [Math]::Pow($radius * $speed, 3) / ($outer * $outer)\n83: $checks += Assert-Near 'Q1 whole radial energy plus outer kinetic flux, W' ($contentKdot + $contentUdot + $exteriorKdot + $shellKdot + $shellUdot + $outerFlux) 0 1e-12\n84: \n85: # Compression: independent midpoint integration of -p dA against state energy.\n86: $compressionArea = 0.81 * $area\n87: $compressionEnergy = Store $compressionArea $area\n88: $integrationWork = 0.0\n89: $recoveryWork = 0.0\n90: $samples = 10000\n91: $deltaArea = ($compressionArea - $area) / $samples\n92: for ($sample = 0; $sample -lt $samples; ++$sample) {\n93:     $integrationWork -= (Pressure ($area + ($sample + 0.5) * $deltaArea) $area) * $deltaArea\n94:     $recoveryWork += (Pressure ($compressionArea - ($sample + 0.5) * $deltaArea) $area) * $deltaArea\n95: }\n96: $checks += Assert-Near 'compression integral -p dA, J' $integrationWork $compressionEnergy 1e-8\n97: $checks += Assert-Near 'recovery integral -p dA, J' $recoveryWork (-$compressionEnergy) 1e-8\n98: \n99: # Zero-storage resistive port thermodynamic step, not a channel-flow simulation.\n100: $duration = 0.1; $conductance = 0.005\n101: $left = 1e-8; $right = 0.01\n102: for ($iteration = 0; $iteration -lt 60; ++$iteration) {\n103:     $transfer = ($left + $right) / 2\n104:     $equation = $transfer - $duration * $conductance * ((Head-Donor $transfer) - (Head-Receiver $transfer))\n105:     if ($equation -gt 0) { $right = $transfer } else { $left = $transfer }\n106: }\n107: $transfer = ($left + $right) / 2\n108: $headA = Head-Donor $transfer; $headB = Head-Receiver $transfer\n109: $dHydraulic = $transfer * ($headA - $headB)\n110: $rate = $transfer / $duration\n111: $portLength = 0.25\n112: $upwindSpeed = $rate / ((1.1 - $transfer / 2) * $portLength)\n113: $receiverSpeed = -0.02\n114: $receiverAfter = (0.9 * $receiverSpeed + $transfer * $upwindSpeed) / (0.9 + $transfer)\n115: $beforeK = 0.5 * 1.1 * $upwindSpeed * $upwindSpeed + 0.5 * 0.9 * $receiverSpeed * $receiverSpeed\n116: $afterK = 0.5 * (1.1 - $transfer) * $upwindSpeed * $upwindSpeed + 0.5 * (0.9 + $transfer) * $receiverAfter * $receiverAfter\n117: $dMix = 0.5 * $transfer * 0.9 / (0.9 + $transfer) * [Math]::Pow($upwindSpeed - $receiverSpeed, 2)\n118: $deltaU = (Store 1 (1.1 - $transfer)) + (Store 1 (0.9 + $transfer)) - (Store 1 1.1) - (Store 1 0.9)\n119: $checks += Assert-Near 'finite rate constitutive residual, kg' ($transfer - $duration * $conductance * ($headA - $headB)) 0 1e-12\n120: $checks += Assert-Near 'finite transfer mass, kg' ((1.1 - $transfer) + (0.9 + $transfer)) 2 1e-12\n121: $checks += Assert-Near 'finite transfer momentum, kg m/s' ((1.1 - $transfer) * $upwindSpeed + (0.9 + $transfer) * $receiverAfter) (1.1 * $upwindSpeed + 0.9 * $receiverSpeed) 1e-12\n122: $checks += Assert-Near 'finite transfer kinetic capture plus dissipation, J' ($afterK - $beforeK + $dMix) 0 1e-12\n123: $checks += Assert-Near 'finite transfer total energy plus physical dissipation, J' ($deltaU + $afterK - $beforeK + $dHydraulic + $dMix) 0 1e-12\n124: $checks += Assert-Near 'finite transfer global tracer, kg' ((1.1 - $transfer) * 0.2 + 0.9 * 0.1 + $transfer * 0.2) (1.1 * 0.2 + 0.9 * 0.1) 1e-12\n125: if ($dHydraulic -lt 0 -or $dMix -lt 0 -or $transfer -ge 0.1) { throw 'Invalid finite exchange or dissipation' }\n126: \n127: $ellipseA = 1.25; $ellipseB = 0.8\n128: $ellipseRatio = ($ellipseA * $ellipseA - $ellipseB * $ellipseB) / ($ellipseA * $ellipseA + $ellipseB * $ellipseB)\n129: $omegaPeak = [Math]::PI * [Math]::PI / 2\n130: $mach = $ellipseRatio * $omegaPeak * $ellipseA / [Math]::Sqrt(10)\n131: if ($mach -le 0.1) { throw 'Expected Q4 reduced-model validity mismatch not detected' }\n132: \n133: if ($Document) {\n134:     $root = Split-Path $PSScriptRoot -Parent\n135:     $docPath = Join-Path $root 'docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md'\n136:     $doc = Get-Content -LiteralPath $docPath -Raw\n137:     foreach ($term in @('F-contents-law', 'UNAPPROVED', 'actual area', 'circulation', 'inertia', 'finite', 'checkpoint', 'resolved interior', 'prefix', 'C-contents', 'C-boundary', 'Q4', 'Q5', 'physical dissipation')) {\n138:         if (-not $doc.Contains($term)) { throw \"Document missing $term\" }\n139:     }\n140:     foreach ($link in [regex]::Matches($doc, '\\]\\(([^)#]+)(?:#[^)]*)?\\)')) {\n141:         $target = $link.Groups[1].Value\n142:         if ($target -notmatch '^https?://' -and -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath (Join-Path (Split-Path $docPath -Parent) $target))) { throw \"Broken local link $target\" }\n143:     }\n144:     if ($doc -match '(?m)[\\t ]+\\r?$') { throw 'Trailing document whitespace' }\n145:     'Document terms, local link targets and whitespace: PASS'\n146: }\n147: $checks | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 3\n148: [pscustomobject]@{\n149:     algebra_checks = $checks.Count; area_m2 = $area; inertia_kg_m2 = $inertia\n150:     translation_K_J = 0.025 * $area; spin_K_J = 0.005 * $inertia\n151:     radial_acceleration_m_s2 = $acceleration; radial_inertia_pressure_N_m = $piWall\n152:     radial_wall_power_W = $wallPower; compression_U_J = $compressionEnergy\n153:     compression_p_N_m = Pressure $compressionArea $area\n154:     transfer_kg = $transfer; rate_kg_s = $rate; donor_head_J_kg = $headA; receiver_head_J_kg = $headB\n155:     donor_port_speed_m_s = $upwindSpeed; receiver_after_m_s = $receiverAfter\n156:     hydraulic_dissipation_J = $dHydraulic; capture_dissipation_J = $dMix\n157:     receiver_tracer_fraction = (0.09 + 0.2 * $transfer) / (0.9 + $transfer)\n158:     Q4_potential_speed_m_s = $mach * [Math]::Sqrt(10); Q4_acoustic_ratio = $mach\n159:     Q4_rigid_inertia_kg_m2 = [Math]::PI * ($ellipseA * $ellipseA + $ellipseB * $ellipseB) / 4\n160:     Q4_irrotational_inertia_kg_m2 = [Math]::PI * [Math]::Pow($ellipseA * $ellipseA - $ellipseB * $ellipseB, 2) / (4 * ($ellipseA * $ellipseA + $ellipseB * $ellipseB))\n161: } | ConvertTo-Json\n",
