                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:4fc99e02a004a262",
                                "lines": "201: fluid costs. This closure might cost too much to serve as cheap contents; only a\n202: complete measured candidate can settle that. There is no performance claim here.\n203: \n204: ## 4. Opening and transfer contract\n205: \n206: ### 4.1 One route and one ledger\n207: \n208: An intact boundary has no route. A port has a stable ID, a real open segment and\n209: length, orientation, source/receiver side, wall velocity, material identity and\n210: law identity. A zero-storage resistive port is a **zero-volume interface** between\n211: adjacent owners. A finite-length channel with appreciable stored mass, delay or\n212: inertia must belong to C-fluid or a separately defined physical contents region;\n213: it cannot be an unowned pipe. This draft does not prescribe its detailed flow law.\n214: \n215: | Entry for a finite exchange | Required meaning |\n216: | --- | --- |\n217: | Revision and interval | Same accepted candidate interval at both owners; consume once on commit |\n218: | Delta q_s, Delta m | Remove donor fractions of the actual accepted mass; add exactly those amounts to receiver. Sum Delta q_s=Delta m |\n219: | Spatial route | Shared interface point/segment and physical side; retain quadrature point application positions for angular momentum |\n220: | Carried impulse | Integral v_port dm, using the donor-side solved velocity in the common inertial frame. Separate port pressure/viscous reaction impulses |\n221: | Carried energy | Integral (e+|v_port|²/2) dm, plus pressure work from the same port state and motion; dissipation and any channel storage separate |\n222: | Receipt | Accepted mass, impulse, angular impulse, storage/work changes and nonnegative physical dissipation; failure invalidates the whole pair |\n223: \n224: At a moving port with normal pointing out of owner a,\n225: `mass_out_rate = integral rho_a (v_a-v_port_wall) dot n dl`.\n226: Pressure work out of a is `integral p_a v_a dot n dl`; separating the moving-wall\n227: part leaves flow work `(p_a/rho_a) mass_out_rate`. At a stationary port the sum of\n228: advected stored energy and flow work is `h_a mass_out_rate`, augmented by\n229: `pi_a/rho_a` when the inertial reaction contributes. Do not post both enthalpy and\n230: the same pressure-flow work. Moving supports also exchange metered impulse/work.\n231: Equal mass rates need not mean equal volume rates when densities differ.\n232: \n233: Port velocity is solved from normal flux, tangential boundary data and the donor\n234: velocity closure, not chosen to make a visually plausible jet. Any velocity change\n235: inside a channel has a paired reaction on its walls and a work/storage/dissipation\n236: account. Noncoincident impulse applications require their physical couple; equal\n237: linear impulses alone do not close angular momentum.\n238: \n239: ### 4.2 Finite conductance, energy and mixing\n240: \n241: For a **quasistatic, stationary, same-material resistive interface** the illustrative\n242: law is `m_dot = G (h_a-h_b)`, G>0 with units kg²/(J s). It relaxes pressure because\n243: h is monotone in density for this material. G is a declared parameter, not inferred\n244: from gap size alone. For a fixed-geometry finite step define\n245: \n246: ```text\n247: h_a^d = [U(Aa,Ma)-U(Aa,Ma-dm)]/dm\n248: h_b^d = [U(Ab,Mb+dm)-U(Ab,Mb)]/dm\n249: dm = dt G (h_a^d-h_b^d)\n250: D_hyd = dm (h_a^d-h_b^d) = dm²/(dt G) >= 0\n251: Delta Ua + Delta Ub + D_hyd = 0\n252: ```\n253: \n254: Use the derivative limit at dm=0 and a bracketed monotone solve (maximum 64\n255: iterations for this proposed example). Require the true mass-equation residual\n256: to be at most 1e-12 kg for the 1 kg reference scale, and at most one hundredth of\n257: the contract's local mass allowance at other scales. For reversed head, exchange\n258: donor and receiver roles and retain the signed posting in the original route;\n259: upwind fractions and velocity always come from the actual donor.\n260: Require the donor, both z ranges and a\n261: proposed 1% maximum donor-mass change per accepted substep. Also bound transfer by\n262: the equal-density mass for the two volumes, so a large step cannot overshoot and\n263: swap their pressure order. Exceeding a bound requests a bounded smaller substep;\n264: it never clips dm after calculating momentum/work. A closed port has G=0 and\n265: dm=0 exactly. Two chambers remain separate inventories and concentrations.\n266: \n267: This law consumes pressure/free-energy difference as explicitly modelled hydraulic\n268: physical dissipation. With no thermal state it is a nonrecoverable sink recorded\n269: in D_physical; it is never returned as fuel. A model intended to predict heating\n270: must carry that thermal state in later scope. The law is not a general inertial\n271: channel law: when kinetic heads, moving supports or pi are material, solve their\n272: momentum and energy balance as well. Reusing the h-only formula for those cases\n273: without those accounts is unsupported.\n274: \n275: Composition mixes **only within the receiver** according to its declared well-mixed\n276: approximation: `(q_s,b+dm Y_s,a)/(Mb+dm)`. It does not set both owners to a shared\n277: concentration. Inlet kinetic energy likewise cannot disappear just because a\n278: receiver stores fewer modes. For a translational capture audit, a packet dm at\n279: velocity w joining mass Mb at velocity Vb gives\n280: \n281: ```text\n282: P_b' = Mb Vb + dm w                  Vb' = P_b'/(Mb+dm)\n283: D_capture = 0.5 dm Mb/(Mb+dm) |w-Vb|²\n284: K_b' - K_b = 0.5 dm |w|² - D_capture\n285: ```\n286: \n287: This is an explicit perfectly inelastic mixing idealization and a ledger test,\n288: not a rule to dissipate every unresolved motion. General receipt must retain\n289: angular momentum and all supported velocity modes, deriving its capture loss\n290: from their positive kinetic metric. A negative required loss, unmatched torque,\n291: unrepresentable circulation or a physically important inlet jet rejects reduced\n292: receipt and requires a resolved interior. No global velocity/energy correction is\n293: allowed to make a transfer pass.\n294: \n295: ### 4.3 Spatial compatibility and the scope of an exchange audit\n296: \n297: For an open chamber uniform-density continuity requires\n298: `div(v)=A_dot/A-M_dot/M`; port normal data include its actual mass flux divided by\n299: that owner's density. The auxiliary solve must satisfy this compatibility, along\n300: with the receiver's momentum and energy ledger, before a coupled step can commit.\n301: In particular, with `j=(v-v_wall) dot n`, the open-chamber first-moment identity is\n302: `P=M c_dot+integral_boundary rho (x-c) j dl`. The sealed identity P=M c_dot\n303: cannot be used unchanged while mass crosses an off-centre port. The potential\n304: now generally has a nonzero mean gradient; compute P from its actual field.\n305: Inflow cannot arbitrarily reset Gamma or spread local vorticity uniformly. Nonzero\n306: Gamma is supported here only in sealed chambers. This draft supports irrotational,\n307: slowly varying inflow only when the closure and\n308: reaction accounts are compatible; vortical injection and rupture jets require\n309: C-fluid. No general vorticity-injection or finite-channel algorithm is claimed.\n310: \n311: The worked exchange below is an exact **isolated exchange-account reference** at\n312: fixed geometry, with a translational capture subproblem. It does not solve the\n313: two chambers' open-boundary spatial flow or certify that a coupled trajectory at\n314: that rate meets this closure's validity limits. Those are separate implementation\n315: checks. A successful packet ledger is necessary and not sufficient for a valid\n316: pressure-driven opening. Finite transport is demonstrated without claiming an\n317: invented homogeneous velocity is a solution of the inlet flow.\n318: \n319: ## 5. Validity, failure and restart\n320: \n321: The density constraint deliberately removes acoustic propagation and spatial\n322: density variations. Proposed screening conditions are\n323: `max |v-c_dot|/c <= 0.1`, `max |pi|/(rho c²) <= 0.01`, and an acoustic crossing\n324: time `diameter/c <= 0.1 T_forcing`. These are conservative initial validity screens,\n325: not physical-error proofs or approvals. Compare to an independent resolved model\n326: at the contract's force/work and trajectory budgets before adopting the reduction.\n327: Chemistry requires a separately justified mixing time at most one tenth the\n328: shortest transfer/reaction time; this draft makes no chemical mixing measurement.\n329: \n330: Use a resolved interior when spatial density/acoustic response, sloshing/free\n331: surfaces, stratification, concentration gradients, jets, circulation structure,\n332: viscous spin-up, local shear transport or channel storage changes the experiment.\n333: Multiple holes, pinching/disconnected chambers and unsupported normal motion are\n334: outside this simply connected closure. Preserve the contract's 0.25 m minimum\n335: feature and other support checks. No automatic representation switching is added;\n336: select supported ownership in the fixture before execution or fail honestly.\n337: \n338: The mass and energy law remains defined beyond the kinetic approximation's\n339: validity; a finite pbar is not evidence that the closure can advance that state.\n340: In particular:\n341: \n342: - Q0 is a rest identity test. Q1/Q2 require radial inertia, not massless contents.\n343: - Q3 acceleration needs the inertial pressure gradient; constant area does not\n344:   eliminate support work.\n345: - Q4 must distinguish initially rotating contents from a wall rotating around\n346:   initially stationary slip contents. The ellipse also challenges the uniform\n347:   density approximation quantitatively (section 6.2).\n348: - Q5 needs the non-affine solve and its measured cost and validity; a scalar radius\n349:   model cannot claim that case. Q6 must preserve the last valid prefix on failure.\n350: \n351: All content/port solves, geometry and mechanics stage together. Recompute actual\n352: area, density, composition, kinetic energy, circulation consistency, local work,\n353: momentum and support/validity diagnostics before commit. Apply F-contract's prefix\n354: commit semantics and retry caps; no missing interval, negative-amount clipping,\n355: unrecorded circulation change, pressure clamp or silent freeze is permitted.\n356: Attempt logs are diagnostic; physical ledgers change only on accepted steps.\n357: \n358: Checkpoint-relevant content state is chamber/material/port IDs and generations,\n359: species amounts, material constants, circulation and closure version, committed\n360: geometry/motion references, actual time, external/port schedule cursor, and\n361: cumulative physical/numerical accounts. Content momentum represented through the\n362: coupled kinetic closure is stored once in that declared generalized-state\n363: representation; derived P, L and K must agree after restore. Rebuild area/moments,\n364: phi and pi deterministically or save solver/mesh history when it changes the\n365: accepted continuation. No uncommitted packets survive a restart. The current\n366: Bitloop hooks provide none of these guarantees yet.\n367: \n368: ## 6. Worked account references\n369: \n370: These are analytic/algebraic checks, not numerical trajectories or qualification.\n371: All cases use binary64; no randomness. The script in section 7 reproduces the\n372: reported numbers. The discrete polygon is used where stated; continuum circles\n373: and ellipses are separate refinement references, not substituted polygon areas.\n374: \n375: ### 6.1 Translation and initialized rotation\n376: \n377: For the N=128, R=1 m base polygon, A=M=3.140331156954753 in m² and kg, and\n378: `trace(Q)=(2+cos(2 pi/N))/6`. I=1.5695351340759076 kg m². At V=(0.2,-0.1) m/s,\n379: P=(0.628066231390951,-0.314033115695475) kg m/s and\n380: Kc=0.07850827892386883 J. At a_c=(0.03,0.04) m/s²,\n381: `pi=-rho a_c dot r`; contents exert wall force\n382: (-0.0942099347086426,-0.125613246278190) N and wall power\n383: -0.006280662313909506 W. The support provides the opposite force/work to contents;\n384: pressure-storage work is zero because A is fixed. Edge quadrature checks both\n385: force components, torque and power independently of the moment formula.\n386: \n387: Initialize actual rigid spin at Omega=0.1/s with Gamma=2 Omega A. Then\n388: L_c=0.156953513407591 kg m²/s and Kc=0.007847675670379538 J.\n389: `pi=rho Omega² (|r|²-trace(Q))/2` balances centripetal acceleration. Its integrated\n390: torque and wall power are zero at constant spin. This energy was supplied in the\n391: initial state; it was not generated by turning a slip wall. With radial scale R\n392: changing and Gamma fixed, Omega varies as R^-2, I as R², so L_c stays constant;\n393: spin energy changes through the computed radial wall work, not a fixed Omega rule.\n394: \n395: ### 6.2 Rotating ellipse and the Q4 routing limit\n396: \n397: For a continuum ellipse with axes a,b rotating at instantaneous angular speed\n398: Omega from irrotational contents, the potential is `phi=k x y` in its aligned\n399: frame, where `k=Omega(a²-b²)/(a²+b²)`. Its normal velocity matches the rotating wall.\n400: Its kinetic rotational coefficient is\n",
                                "next_offset": 400,
                                "path": "docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md",
                                "total_lines": 547
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "arguments": {
                                "action": "read",
                                "limit": 160,
