                            },
                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:4fc99e02a004a262",
                                "lines": "401: `I_irrot=M(a²-b²)²/[4(a²+b²)]`; the rigid-spin coefficient is\n402: `I_rigid=M(a²+b²)/4`. They describe different states and must not be interchanged.\n403: The pressure reaction, including derivatives of the rotating frame, supplies\n404: torque and work. A circle has I_irrot=0 while its initialized rigid-spin inertia\n405: remains M R²/2.\n406: \n407: At a=1.25 m, b=0.8 m, M=pi kg these coefficients are\n408: 0.303463675727469 and 1.72983945488288 kg m². The specified Q4 period of 2 s gives\n409: Omega_peak=pi²/2 per second. The irrotational velocity has peak magnitude\n410: `|k| a = 2.5836294154384025 m/s`; c=sqrt(10)=3.16227766017 m/s, giving 0.8170153583,\n411: well above the proposed 0.1 screening limit. A crossing-time ratio is also too\n412: large (2.5/c divided by 2 s is about 0.395, above 0.1).\n413: \n414: **Decision-bearing limitation:** the existing Q4 ellipse cannot be qualified by\n415: this low-acoustic-speed-variation reduction under these proposed validity screens.\n416: This is an analytic input/assumption mismatch, not a measured density error or a\n417: failed runtime solver. Keep the fixture unchanged; a review must select a suitable\n418: resolved interior (including compressibility if necessary), or explicitly approve\n419: changed scales after assessing the physical budgets. Slowing the fixture silently\n420: or interpreting wall rotation as fluid spin would conceal the mismatch.\n421: \n422: ### 6.3 Free expansion\n423: \n424: Use F-contract's circular Q1 oracle: M=1.1 pi kg, shell mass 0.2 pi kg, R0=1 m,\n425: outer reservoir radius 4 m, exterior rho=1 kg/m² and edge coefficient S=1 N.\n426: At R=1.02 m and R_dot=0.1 m/s, the contract's coupled radial equation gives\n427: R_ddot=0.3089800747511852 m/s². Interior `v=(R_dot/R) r`,\n428: Kc=M R_dot²/4 and `pi_wall=-rho R R_ddot/4=-0.08330345152605485 N/m`.\n429: Thus the net outward content wall power is 0.31374721716642046 W and\n430: `Kc_dot+U_dot+W_wall_dot=0` to 5.56e-17 W in the audit.\n431: The inertia term is exactly the M/2 term in the contract's radial equation;\n432: adding another M/2 wall mass would double count it.\n433: \n434: For completeness the independent audit differentiates the shell elastic and\n435: kinetic energy and exterior kinetic energy `pi rho_f (R R_dot)² ln(Ro/R)`.\n436: Adding the outer advected kinetic flux `pi rho_f (R R_dot)³/Ro²` leaves\n437: 1.32e-17 W. No reservoir kinetic outflow is discarded. This is a point identity,\n438: not a time integration or evidence of stable free expansion over its horizon.\n439: \n440: ### 6.4 Compression and recovery\n441: \n442: Use the actual base polygon with M=A0 kg and prescribed Q2 radius from 1 to\n443: 0.9 m. The area becomes 0.81 A0, not 0.9 A0. At the turning point velocity is\n444: zero; pbar=2.3456790123456783 N/m and U=0.6507090024477902 J. The work received by\n445: contents from the mean pressure is `-integral pbar dA=+0.6507090024477902 J`.\n446: An independent 10,000-panel midpoint integral agrees within 2.48e-10 J.\n447: Recovery returns that compression store to zero. During motion retain\n448: `Kc=M trace(Q0) R_dot²/2` and the corresponding pi work; endpoints at rest do not\n449: justify dropping transient inertia. Ideal complete-cycle content work is zero;\n450: structure/exterior/support accounts still belong to the full Q2 experiment.\n451: \n452: ### 6.5 Connected finite-rate exchange and capture ledger\n453: \n454: Take two distinct fixed-area owners Aa=Ab=1 m², Ma=1.1 kg, Mb=0.9 kg, with the\n455: same material, tracer fractions 0.2 and 0.1, an adjacent resistive interface,\n456: G=0.005 kg²/(J s), and dt=0.1 s. These are additional proposed reference values,\n457: not a change to the passive sealed fixtures. Solve section 4.2's finite equation:\n458: \n459: | Observation | Value |\n460: | --- | --- |\n461: | dm, m_dot | 0.000998311836412136 kg; 0.00998311836412136 kg/s |\n462: | Donor/receiver energy secants | 0.948562643580380 and -1.048061029243595 J/kg |\n463: | D_hyd, equal to minus total compression-energy change | 0.001993253045440846 J |\n464: | Receiver tracer fraction after receipt | 0.10011080063339711; donor stays 0.2 |\n465: \n466: Only about 0.001 kg moves; the equal-density amount would be 0.1 kg. Neither\n467: pressures nor concentrations become identical in this finite interval. Negative\n468: receiver h is permitted by the fixed energy reference and does not reverse the\n469: positive hydraulic dissipation. Total mass remains exactly 2 kg in the audit;\n470: species postings remove/add the same donor fractions.\n471: \n472: For the companion one-dimensional impulse/capture ledger, use port length 0.25 m\n473: and mean donor density 1.1-dm/2. The **flux-derived** upstream speed is\n474: `w=m_dot/(rho_a,mean length)=0.036318729241025284 m/s`. Let the donor translational\n475: mode have this speed and the receiver mode initially -0.02 m/s. The receiver's\n476: post-capture mode is -0.019937598491279733 m/s. Post dm*w at the same interface\n477: point to both owners with opposite signs; take centres/route/velocities collinear\n478: on x so that angular momentum about the origin is zero. The removed donor kinetic\n479: energy is dm*w²/2; the receiver stores that amount minus\n480: D_capture=1.5814681532316898e-6 J. Combined momentum residual is 3.47e-18 kg m/s.\n481: \n482: The donor's combined stored/flow-energy removal is dm*h_a^d and the receiver's\n483: compression-energy gain is dm*h_b^d; the difference is the declared resistive loss.\n484: Together with kinetic capture,\n485: `Delta(Ua+Ub+Ka+Kb)+D_hyd+D_capture=0` to 1.67e-19 J. These are separate physical\n486: loss mechanisms and neither is a numerical residual. Application to a real\n487: moving/jet-producing connector needs its additional reaction work and spatial\n488: compatibility in section 4.3; this account example claims no such trajectory.\n489: \n490: ## 7. Verification record and implementation gates\n491: \n492: Relevant capability IDs used: **bitloop** (ownership and diagnostic boundary) and\n493: **simulation-health** (local/global oracles, honest horizon classification).\n494: Read the local skill and routed health guidance; no Bitloop runtime capability was\n495: needed for this document task. No package, build preset or existing source changed.\n496: \n497: Reproduce the specification arithmetic and document checks from the project root:\n498: \n499: ```powershell\n500: ./tooling/check_contents_law.ps1 -Document\n501: git diff --check\n502: ```\n503: \n504: The [audit script](../tooling/check_contents_law.ps1) is a standalone binary64\n505: reference calculation, not production simulation code. Its 20 algebra checks\n506: cover differentiated storage, independent polygon edge integration, radial work,\n507: compression quadrature and finite exchange/capture. Audit tolerances are 1e-8 in\n508: the indicated units for finite differences/quadrature and 1e-12 for exact\n509: identities; they are arithmetic-check tolerances, not substitute physical budgets.\n510: Observed pressure and mass-energy derivative errors were 1.12e-10 N/m and\n511: 8.03e-11 J/kg. All 20 checks passed after correcting a PowerShell array-expression\n512: parenthesis in the checker. Required document terms, local link targets and\n513: whitespace are also checked. Compact decisive outputs are retained in section 6;\n514: there was no physical raw run to archive or simulated horizon to report.\n515: \n516: F-contract's local and cumulative budgets, dt/refinement matrix and behavioral\n517: horizons remain the gates for an implementation. For sealed mechanics use its\n518: 2 s rejection screen, 20 then 40 s behavior windows, and stated extensions;\n519: include a zero-circulation spinning circle and the ellipse state distinction.\n520: For an eventual opening experiment cover at least ten relaxation times\n521: `tau ~= 1/[G (partial h_a/partial Ma + partial h_b/partial Mb)]`, plus onset,\n522: closure and flow reversal, and extend while inventory/work drift is unresolved.\n523: For the equal 1 kg, 1 m² owners above the linearized tau is 10 s. Freeze approved\n524: scales and validity limits first; do not run an unsupported case and call its\n525: continued numerical motion physical acceptance.\n526: \n527: Before adoption also demonstrate non-affine Q5, compatible open-port spatial\n528: flow, local inertial traction/flux refinement, conservation under failed attempts,\n529: restart continuation, and complete cost including the auxiliary inertia solves.\n530: Those are future implementation/qualification work, not tests asserted to have\n531: run for this specification. Reserved active/fuel/sensing interfaces remain as in\n532: F-contract; this law supplies no active energy from numerical or hydraulic losses.\n533: \n534: | T-design-contents acceptance | Location |\n535: | --- | --- |\n536: | Geometry/inventory pressure, storage, mass/inertia, forces and paired work | Sections 1-3 |\n537: | Translation/rotation, expansion, compression and finite-rate reference accounts | Section 6 and executable algebra audit |\n538: | Opening amount/momentum/energy ownership and validity limits | Sections 4-5 |\n539: \n540: Standard continuum background for the control-volume work terms is Richard\n541: Fitzpatrick's [Energy Conservation](https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336L/Fluidhtml/node14.html).\n542: His [Circulation and Vorticity](https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336L/Fluidhtml/node62.html)\n543: and [Irrotational Flow](https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336L/Fluidhtml/node64.html)\n544: explain the circulation and potential-flow identities. The uniform-density\n545: closure, project barotrope, finite secants, validity screens and numerical values\n546: here are project-specific proposals/derivations, not results certified by those\n547: references.\n",
                                "next_offset": null,
