                                "lines": "1: # F-boundary-law: sided moving geometry and cut-cell exchanges\n2: \n3: Draft 1, 2026-10-10. MindGraph task **T-design-boundary**.\n4: \n5: This specifies one candidate: explicit sided polygon cuts, space-time swept-area\n6: accounting, and conservative same-side control-volume agglomeration. It is a\n7: design for the existing owners, not another simulation or a qualified solver.\n8: **All added thresholds, caps and example parameters are PROPOSED / UNAPPROVED.**\n9: Inherited numerical proposals remain unapproved. No fixture, material law or\n10: acceptance budget in the inputs is changed.\n11: \n12: ## 1. Inputs, ownership and compatibility\n13: \n14: Inspected inputs are [F-contract draft 1.1](PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md),\n15: [F-mechanics-law](MECHANICS_LAW.md), [F-contents-law](COMPARTMENT_LAW.md), and\n16: [F-flow-law](STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md). Their SHA-256 identities are retained in\n17: section 9. The [current scene](../Substrate/Substrate.cpp) has diagnostic tick/time\n18: counters only and checkpoint hooks returning false. There is no cut-cell runtime\n19: to test. `bitloop_new_project(Substrate ...)` is the application entry in\n20: [CMakeLists.txt](../CMakeLists.txt); this task requires no build or dependency change.\n21: \n22: | Owner | Inputs/outputs at the coupling boundary |\n23: | --- | --- |\n24: | C-mechanics | Owns oriented structural topology, positions, shape functions, structural momentum and the proposed continuous wall trajectory; receives integrated loads at the same physical points |\n25: | C-contents | Owns compartment amounts, circulation/kinetic closure and compression energy; evaluates pressure from the actual polygon area and returns mean plus inertial traction |\n26: | C-fluid | Owns resolved primal inventories and component momenta, performs transport/viscosity/pressure solves and conservative remaps; no resolved inventory occupies a well-mixed chamber |\n27: | C-boundary | Derives fragments, aperture intervals, side connectivity, area/moment integrals, sweeps, aggregation maps and force/work maps; owns no material, momentum or energy reservoir |\n28: | C-world | Stages one candidate, settles paired exchanges, validates, commits accepted prefixes and preserves actual committed time after failure |\n29: \n30: Lengths are m, area is m², density kg/m², pressure N/m, force N, impulse kg m/s\n31: and work J. There is no hidden thickness. A thin membrane excludes zero area but\n32: has two distinct sides. Tissue and solid supports exclude their actual area.\n33: Stable structural/material/domain/support IDs and generations identify physical\n34: objects. Cut fragments, aggregate rows and dual-volume indices are derived,\n35: revision-tagged objects, not permanent material IDs.\n36: \n37: This design retains the mechanics law's midpoint trajectory and dry contact\n38: limits, the contents law's real inertia and pressure law, and the flow law's\n39: staggered momentum, donor transport, symmetric backward-Euler viscosity and\n40: PCG/IC(0) projection. Sections 3–5 specify the additional geometric operations\n41: needed for moving cuts; stationary full cells reduce to the flow specification.\n42: \n43: Two existing negative predictions remain decision-bearing:\n44: \n45: * The supplemental no-slip channel of width 0.25 m at h=1/16 m has predicted\n46:   flow/work error 12.5% and pointwise velocity error 6.25%, exceeding the flow\n47:   law's proposed 2% and 0.5% gates. This is not a measured error for free-slip Q5.\n48: * Q4's rotating ellipse requires 2.583629415 m/s in the contents law's irrotational\n49:   reference, giving speed/c=0.817015358 versus its proposed 0.1 validity screen.\n50:   Geometry conservation cannot make that reduction valid.\n51: \n52: The stationary incompressible law also does not provide an absolute pressure or\n53: compressibility closure for a sealed, volume-changing resolved interior. Such an\n54: interior is **unsupported by this combination of laws**. Do not use a pressure\n55: gauge as a wall load or convert the chamber to a resolved region automatically.\n56: Fixed-wall internal flow remains supported as specified by F-flow-law. A suitable\n57: physical interior closure or a reviewed fixture change precedes qualification of\n58: the incompatible rotating/volume-changing cases; neither is supplied here.\n59: \n60: ## 2. Sided geometry and the swept-area identity\n61: \n62: ### 2.1 Cuts and connectivity\n63: \n64: Intersect the actual structural segments and analytic support boundaries with\n65: each Cartesian primal cell and each shifted x/y momentum dual cell. A fragment\n66: is one connected fluid portion, including its material/domain and barrier-side\n67: incidences. A cell can contain several fragments. Store oriented boundary loops,\n68: area, centroid and second moments, wall facet provenance, and ordered open\n69: intervals on Cartesian faces. Use polygon moment integrals; integrate analytic\n70: capsule arcs consistently for both collision and fluid geometry. An independently\n71: bounded arc approximation is acceptable only if collision uses that identical\n72: approximation and its error fits the contract's geometric budgets.\n73: \n74: Pair an open face interval only with the geometrically coincident interval in\n75: the neighbouring cell and the same physical fluid domain. Positive length and\n76: an unobstructed path are required. A point or corner touching is not connectivity.\n77: Matching component names alone are insufficient: opposite sides of a membrane\n78: must not acquire an edge even if fluid can reach the other side around a distant\n79: tip. Closed chamber loops are watertight. No pressure, transport, viscous,\n80: reconstruction or preconditioner stencil may tunnel through a barrier.\n81: \n82: Use filtered orientation/intersection predicates with a bounded exact-predicate\n83: fallback, deterministic tie ownership at grid lines, and explicit degenerate\n84: event handling. Never snap two distinct walls together to simplify clipping.\n85: If classification cannot establish sided connectivity, reject with the affected\n86: stable IDs and grid coordinates. Zero-area bookkeeping records may represent an\n87: event, but cannot own inventory or become a solver denominator.\n88: \n89: ### 2.2 One trajectory, one sweep\n90: \n91: Use the same candidate trajectory as mechanics. Free vertices use its linear\n92: path q(s)=q0+s(q1-q0); prescribed supports use their declared continuous motion.\n93: Split integration at facet/grid incidence events. Bounds are 64 event roots per\n94: affected cell per attempt and 64 root refinements per root. Ambiguous/coincident\n95: events are processed as a batch; exhausted bounds reject, never omit a sweep.\n96: \n97: For a fixed Cartesian cell, n is outward from its fluid fragment. Define:\n98: \n99: ```text\n100: S_i = integral_t integral_moving_wall w dot n dl dt = A_i1 - A_i0\n101: F_ij = integral_t integral_open_face u dot n_i dl dt = -F_ji\n102: sum_j F_ij + S_i = 0                            (constant-density GCL)\n103: M_i1 = M_i0 - sum_j mass_flux_ij                 (sealed wall flux is zero)\n104: ```\n105: \n106: F and S are areas, not masses. Internal cut-support boundaries are also included\n107: when a fragment changes identity. Follow their incidence through the event or\n108: use the aggregate space-time union from section 3. A fragment birth/death is not\n109: creation/destruction of physical material.\n110: \n111: With uniform density, mass_flux=rho F gives M_i1=rho A_i1. **Do not also post\n112: rho S as a wall mass transfer.** That double-counts displacement. A sealed wall\n113: has exactly zero relative constituent flux by topology. Open Cartesian faces\n114: carry displaced fluid; physical opening transfers, if present, use one explicit\n115: finite packet with the contents law's amount, momentum and enthalpy accounting.\n116: An opening is never inferred from a small cut fraction or solver difficulty.\n117: \n118: Compute S by integrating the moving facets and independently compare endpoint\n119: areas. For a linearly moving polygon, whole-loop area is quadratic in s, so its\n120: midpoint area gradient integrates exactly. Within a grid cell, incidence changes\n121: require event splitting; an old normal times an old length is not sufficient.\n122: Geometric changes are shared by inventories, pressure, reconstruction and images.\n123: \n124: ## 3. One small-cell treatment: conservative same-side agglomeration\n125: \n126: ### 3.1 Selection and bounded size\n127: \n128: Agglomerate primal fragments whose minimum area along the attempted trajectory\n129: is below theta h², with theta=0.25. Apply the same rule independently to each\n130: component's momentum dual fragments. Include fragments that appear/disappear in\n131: the attempted interval. Select neighbours through positive-length open apertures\n132: only, in descending minimum aperture measure, with grid/side IDs breaking ties.\n133: No edge across a sealed wall, solid, point contact or different inventory domain\n134: is eligible. An aggregate must remain connected throughout every open event\n135: interval and have area at least theta h² throughout. Endpoint connectivity at\n136: zero-measure events is interpreted by its one-sided limits.\n137: \n138: Limit an aggregate to 8 Cartesian cells and diameter 3h. If a valid group cannot\n139: be made within those limits, return `unsupported_small_region`. An isolated fluid\n140: pocket is not deleted, merged across a wall or declared well-mixed. A region\n141: whose total volume vanishes cannot be advanced as incompressible sealed fluid.\n142: \n143: Freeze the selected union for the whole candidate interval. Preserve it between\n144: accepted steps while valid; permit dissolution only when each remaining fragment\n145: stays above 0.5h² over the next trial. This hysteresis reduces gratuitous regrouping;\n146: it does not waive continuity tests or authorize stale adjacency. Rebuild if any\n147: side, aperture or support revision changes. Topology invariants are checked before\n148: and after aggregation and on every geometry event.\n149: \n150: This is the sole stabilization. There is no volume clipping, artificial wall\n151: permeability, density reset, pressure diagonal shift or fallback redistribution\n152: through arbitrary neighbours. C-world's existing bounded timestep retries remain\n153: available but are not a second representation.\n154: \n155: ### 3.2 Inventories and support transitions\n156: \n157: Store each aggregate's constituent amounts once in C-fluid. The aggregate area is\n158: the sum of its actual fragments; local reconstructed amount is q_i=q_G A_i/A_G.\n159: This reconstruction is piecewise constant and explicitly introduces subgrid\n160: mixing **within that connected same-side aggregate**. The aggregate length scale\n161: and this loss of concentration resolution must be reported. A narrow biological\n162: gradient cannot be qualified merely because its total amount is conserved.\n163: \n164: External aggregate faces use one antisymmetric donor flux. Internal faces cancel\n165: from the aggregate update. Enforce the flow law's outgoing mass fraction <=0.5\n166: on each primal aggregate and reject negative constituent amounts without clipping.\n167: Retain a conservative fine-fragment flux reconstruction\n168: for local geometric/oracle checks: prescribe the fragment endpoint amounts above,\n169: compute each fragment's deficit after its external fluxes, and route the deficits\n170: on a deterministic spanning tree of actual internal open apertures. A leaf posts\n171: its deficit on its unique parent edge and is removed; the final root deficit must\n172: be zero to roundoff. Route separately on event intervals where connectivity differs.\n173: This is a diagnostic/resolution of the aggregate balance, not a second material\n174: update. Record internal mixing amounts and distinguish them from physical channel\n175: transport. Do not claim a resolved fine velocity from this prolongation alone.\n176: \n177: Before changing aggregation at a fixed physical instant, remap by actual same-side\n178: intersection areas. For old group a and new group b, transfer\n179: `q_(a->b)=q_a area(a intersect b)/area(a)`, once per constituent. Intersection\n180: columns partition every old group; rows partition the new support. Check these\n181: partitions before posting. A disappearing fine fragment's amounts remain in its\n182: connected aggregate or leave through accounted open faces; no final deletion is\n183: allowed. Newly uncovered support is supplied by that same update, never filled\n184: from a default rho or a remote nearest cell. A group that reaches zero area with\n185: any nonzero amount rejects. No transfer to a geometrically disconnected newborn\n186: group is manufactured.\n187: \n188: The local fine-fragment divergence test uses both actual external fluxes and the\n189: explicit internal prolongation; also check the independent reconstructed velocity\n190: normal trace on each physical wall. A good aggregate residual alone is insufficient.\n191: \n192: ### 3.3 Momentum and interpolation compatibility\n193: \n194: Keep the x and y staggered momentum partitions distinct. For component dual group\n195: d derive its inertial mass from primary inventories with overlap weights:\n196: \n197: ```text\n198: w_di = area(dual_d intersect primal_i) / area(primal_i)\n199: m_d = sum_i w_di M_i;      sum_d w_di = 1 for each component separately.\n200: P_d = m_d u_d;             K = sum_d P_d²/(2m_d).\n",
                                "next_offset": 200,
