                                "lines": "281: \n282: ### 4.2 Moving extension of the stationary flow stages\n283: \n284: Freeze candidate geometry/maps within each outer coupling pass. Set\n285: `q_wall=S/delta`; include known reservoir velocity fluxes in q_N. On these maps,\n286: use the flow law's projection equations with `q_N=q_wall+q_known`:\n287: \n288: ```text\n289: A_p = B M_d^-1 B^T\n290: b = -(B u* + q_N)/delta + B M_d^-1 b_p\n291: u+ = u* + delta M_d^-1 (B^T p - b_p)\n292: Delta K - Wp = -0.5 ||u+-u*||²_Md + delta p^T(Bu+ + q_N)\n293: Wp = -delta (b_p^T u+ + p^T q_N).\n294: ```\n295: \n296: This identity applies with M_d fixed **within that pressure stage**. Account mass\n297: changes, transport and remapping in their own stages. The wall term is `-p^T S`,\n298: exactly the opposite of the work posted to mechanics. The remaining pressure\n299: projection loss is numerical, and an incomplete solve leaves the displayed\n300: residual; neither may be converted into material energy.\n301: \n302: Moving geometry requires an additional kinematic predictor before the stationary\n303: law's donor transport. Project the old velocity onto the above swept continuity\n304: constraint, using homogeneous pressure-increment data at pressure reservoirs.\n305: Transport with that compatible predictor's open-face fluxes. Then apply body\n306: loads, backward-Euler viscosity and the ordinary full-pressure projection, using\n307: the same geometry and q_wall and the updated derived masses. Both projection\n308: impulses are real changes to fluid momentum and both post their respective wall\n309: impulse/work; do not retain only the second load. Their numerical losses are\n310: reported separately. In stationary full cells with a valid divergence-free old\n311: state, the predictor correction is zero and the original ordering is recovered.\n312: \n313: The staged velocity representation and divergence tests refer to these space-time\n314: apertures. Endpoint wall traces are reconstructed with the actual endpoint wall\n315: velocity; check their relative normal error separately. Do not describe an old\n316: geometry divergence or a final image as evidence for the swept flux condition.\n317: Splitting and time-level errors must converge under the contract's temporal\n318: refinement tests. No extra unaccounted final velocity projection is permitted.\n319: \n320: Rerun the whole pass from committed state if the received fluid/content loads\n321: change the mechanical trajectory. Do not accumulate discarded pass impulses or\n322: settle fuel during trial passes. At most four outer passes; require geometry,\n323: loads, local work accounts and committed-state validity simultaneously. A valid\n324: geometric update alone cannot commit while a coupled solver is unconverged.\n325: \n326: ### 4.3 Nullspaces and limits\n327: \n328: Use PCG/IC(0), deterministic ordering and true residuals as in F-flow-law. Each\n329: projection is bounded by the remaining part of a shared 200-iteration allowance\n330: per coupling pass, preserving the contract's cap (at most 800 pressure iterations\n331: per attempted substep). The two projections do not receive separate allowances.\n332: The flux-lift solve has its separate 200-iteration cap per component. No diagonal\n333: shift or fallback solver is implied. Check rank/compatibility before solving.\n334: Each closed connected component has its own constant nullspace; net prescribed\n335: sweep plus exterior flux must be compatible. A sealed incompressible cavity with\n336: nonzero net area change fails, even if deleting its smallest cell would allow a\n337: linear solve. Open environmental reservoirs supply the finite displaced outflow.\n338: Pressure gauges in sealed internal domains do not supply absolute wall pressure.\n339: \n340: ## 5. Collision agreement, continuity and failure\n341: \n342: Use the very same structural segments, capsule surfaces, poses and swept paths\n343: for collision and fluid barriers. Continuous collision checks prevent crossing\n344: between endpoints; endpoint separation alone is insufficient. Wet clearances\n345: must stay at least **0.25 m** at all times, including capsule corners and rotating\n346: features. This physical limit stays fixed on grid refinement. Mechanics' dry\n347: contact treatment does not authorize fluid-gap closure, drainage, lubrication or\n348: wet impact. Reject before crossing the supported gap. No smoothing, aggregation\n349: or tolerance-based surface snap may move the actual collision surface.\n350: \n351: Inherit the contract's displacement <=0.25h, fractional chamber area change <=1%,\n352: four halvings, 512 attempts per H=0.02 s and prefix-commit semantics. Geometry\n353: events inside an attempt are integration pieces, not additional committed time.\n354: All failed attempts leave committed inventories, momentum, geometry, allocator,\n355: time and physical accounts unchanged. Retain previously accepted substeps and\n356: report their actual time. Return the first decisive reason and offending IDs:\n357: `ambiguous_cut`, `unsupported_small_region`, `unsupported_wet_gap`,\n358: `incompatible_component_volume`, `nonconverged_pressure`, `invalid_reconstruction`,\n359: `unsettled_exchange`, or an inherited mechanics/contents validity failure.\n360: \n361: Retain aggregation hysteresis and accepted pressure/velocity reconstruction state\n362: in explicit checkpoints if they affect continuation, plus source/configuration\n363: identities and the physical states required by the input laws. Derived geometry\n364: may be rebuilt only by an identified deterministic procedure. Checkpoint hooks\n365: are not implemented by this document.\n366: \n367: Required continuity tests for implementation:\n368: \n369: 1. Translate the same membrane and corner across cell faces/vertices with offsets\n370:    epsilon/h=1e-2, 1e-4, 1e-6, and the exact incidence event, in both directions.\n371:    Compare one-sided limits of local amounts, force, torque, pressure and work.\n372:    Repeat with a rotation and with reversed structural/grid enumeration.\n373: 2. Sweep cut fractions across theta=0.25 and dissolution=0.5 while holding the\n374:    physical trajectory fixed. Compare forced valid alternative grouping histories.\n375:    Conservation is required for each; differences in observable loads and fields\n376:    remain within the contract's budgets and decrease under refinement. No fixed\n377:    jump can be excused as a bookkeeping change.\n378: 3. Keep two differently tagged species on opposite sides of a translating\n379:    membrane. Check every route, every constituent posting and independent wall\n380:    normal traces. Zero signed global leakage does not pass a two-way leak.\n",
                                "next_offset": 380,
