                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:4fc99e02a004a262",
                                "lines": "171: geometry/time derivative of the closure and its coupling to wall acceleration.\n172: Do not apply a second lumped-mass reaction in addition to pi.\n173: \n174: ### 3.3 Numerical realization and limits on its cost\n175: \n176: For a reference realization select continuous quadratic potential functions on a\n177: deterministic conforming triangulation of the actual polygon. Integrate polynomial\n178: moments exactly to their required degree. Solve the mean-constrained weak form\n179: `integral grad(phi) dot grad(psi) = integral_boundary g psi - integral d psi`.\n180: It reproduces the affine examples without inventing interior mass nodes. Use\n181: matrix-free or sparse assembled projected CG with diagonal scaling, measured true\n182: residuals and the contract's bounded iteration policy. This is a proposed reference\n183: discretization, not a dependency request or an implementation in this task.\n184: \n185: Recover pi by the corresponding weak gradient equation. Recompute the full vector\n186: defect `rho a + grad(pi)` and normal-velocity defect independently; a least-squares\n187: pressure projection cannot conceal incompatible acceleration/curl. Enforce the\n188: contract's pressure/flux and work budgets, refining this auxiliary mesh separately\n189: from the exterior grid. Report quadrature, mesh and time errors independently.\n190: Quadratic C0 potentials have discontinuous element gradients: evaluate acceleration\n191: and traction in a consistent weak form including element-interface terms, not by\n192: treating a broken gradient as globally smooth. The discrete kinetic-energy\n193: derivative and posted wall work must still agree to the contract's budgets.\n194: Use a fixed maximum 200 iterations per solve and 4 coupling passes per attempt,\n195: inherited as unapproved caps; failure cannot launch an unlimited fallback solver.\n196: Degenerate triangles or loss of simple connected geometry reject the attempt.\n197: \n198: All auxiliary assembly, mesh maintenance, geometry derivatives, solves, staging,\n199: checks and retries belong to complete-work timing and memory. Report unknowns,\n200: iterations, residuals, retained matrices, mesh and scratch alongside exterior\n201: fluid costs. This closure might cost too much to serve as cheap contents; only a\n202: complete measured candidate can settle that. There is no performance claim here.\n203: \n204: ## 4. Opening and transfer contract\n205: \n206: ### 4.1 One route and one ledger\n207: \n208: An intact boundary has no route. A port has a stable ID, a real open segment and\n209: length, orientation, source/receiver side, wall velocity, material identity and\n210: law identity. A zero-storage resistive port is a **zero-volume interface** between\n211: adjacent owners. A finite-length channel with appreciable stored mass, delay or\n212: inertia must belong to C-fluid or a separately defined physical contents region;\n213: it cannot be an unowned pipe. This draft does not prescribe its detailed flow law.\n214: \n215: | Entry for a finite exchange | Required meaning |\n216: | --- | --- |\n217: | Revision and interval | Same accepted candidate interval at both owners; consume once on commit |\n218: | Delta q_s, Delta m | Remove donor fractions of the actual accepted mass; add exactly those amounts to receiver. Sum Delta q_s=Delta m |\n219: | Spatial route | Shared interface point/segment and physical side; retain quadrature point application positions for angular momentum |\n220: | Carried impulse | Integral v_port dm, using the donor-side solved velocity in the common inertial frame. Separate port pressure/viscous reaction impulses |\n221: | Carried energy | Integral (e+|v_port|²/2) dm, plus pressure work from the same port state and motion; dissipation and any channel storage separate |\n222: | Receipt | Accepted mass, impulse, angular impulse, storage/work changes and nonnegative physical dissipation; failure invalidates the whole pair |\n223: \n224: At a moving port with normal pointing out of owner a,\n225: `mass_out_rate = integral rho_a (v_a-v_port_wall) dot n dl`.\n226: Pressure work out of a is `integral p_a v_a dot n dl`; separating the moving-wall\n227: part leaves flow work `(p_a/rho_a) mass_out_rate`. At a stationary port the sum of\n228: advected stored energy and flow work is `h_a mass_out_rate`, augmented by\n229: `pi_a/rho_a` when the inertial reaction contributes. Do not post both enthalpy and\n230: the same pressure-flow work. Moving supports also exchange metered impulse/work.\n231: Equal mass rates need not mean equal volume rates when densities differ.\n232: \n233: Port velocity is solved from normal flux, tangential boundary data and the donor\n234: velocity closure, not chosen to make a visually plausible jet. Any velocity change\n235: inside a channel has a paired reaction on its walls and a work/storage/dissipation\n236: account. Noncoincident impulse applications require their physical couple; equal\n237: linear impulses alone do not close angular momentum.\n238: \n239: ### 4.2 Finite conductance, energy and mixing\n240: \n241: For a **quasistatic, stationary, same-material resistive interface** the illustrative\n242: law is `m_dot = G (h_a-h_b)`, G>0 with units kg²/(J s). It relaxes pressure because\n243: h is monotone in density for this material. G is a declared parameter, not inferred\n244: from gap size alone. For a fixed-geometry finite step define\n245: \n246: ```text\n247: h_a^d = [U(Aa,Ma)-U(Aa,Ma-dm)]/dm\n248: h_b^d = [U(Ab,Mb+dm)-U(Ab,Mb)]/dm\n249: dm = dt G (h_a^d-h_b^d)\n250: D_hyd = dm (h_a^d-h_b^d) = dm²/(dt G) >= 0\n251: Delta Ua + Delta Ub + D_hyd = 0\n252: ```\n253: \n254: Use the derivative limit at dm=0 and a bracketed monotone solve (maximum 64\n255: iterations for this proposed example). Require the true mass-equation residual\n256: to be at most 1e-12 kg for the 1 kg reference scale, and at most one hundredth of\n257: the contract's local mass allowance at other scales. For reversed head, exchange\n258: donor and receiver roles and retain the signed posting in the original route;\n259: upwind fractions and velocity always come from the actual donor.\n260: Require the donor, both z ranges and a\n261: proposed 1% maximum donor-mass change per accepted substep. Also bound transfer by\n262: the equal-density mass for the two volumes, so a large step cannot overshoot and\n263: swap their pressure order. Exceeding a bound requests a bounded smaller substep;\n264: it never clips dm after calculating momentum/work. A closed port has G=0 and\n265: dm=0 exactly. Two chambers remain separate inventories and concentrations.\n266: \n267: This law consumes pressure/free-energy difference as explicitly modelled hydraulic\n268: physical dissipation. With no thermal state it is a nonrecoverable sink recorded\n269: in D_physical; it is never returned as fuel. A model intended to predict heating\n270: must carry that thermal state in later scope. The law is not a general inertial\n",
                                "next_offset": 270,
                                "path": "docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md",
                                "total_lines": 547
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "arguments": {
                                "action": "read",
                                "limit": 46,
                                "offset": 318,
                                "path": "docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md",
                                "text": ""
                            },
                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:4fc99e02a004a262",
                                "lines": "319: ## 5. Validity, failure and restart\n320: \n321: The density constraint deliberately removes acoustic propagation and spatial\n322: density variations. Proposed screening conditions are\n323: `max |v-c_dot|/c <= 0.1`, `max |pi|/(rho c²) <= 0.01`, and an acoustic crossing\n324: time `diameter/c <= 0.1 T_forcing`. These are conservative initial validity screens,\n325: not physical-error proofs or approvals. Compare to an independent resolved model\n326: at the contract's force/work and trajectory budgets before adopting the reduction.\n327: Chemistry requires a separately justified mixing time at most one tenth the\n328: shortest transfer/reaction time; this draft makes no chemical mixing measurement.\n329: \n330: Use a resolved interior when spatial density/acoustic response, sloshing/free\n331: surfaces, stratification, concentration gradients, jets, circulation structure,\n332: viscous spin-up, local shear transport or channel storage changes the experiment.\n333: Multiple holes, pinching/disconnected chambers and unsupported normal motion are\n334: outside this simply connected closure. Preserve the contract's 0.25 m minimum\n335: feature and other support checks. No automatic representation switching is added;\n336: select supported ownership in the fixture before execution or fail honestly.\n337: \n338: The mass and energy law remains defined beyond the kinetic approximation's\n339: validity; a finite pbar is not evidence that the closure can advance that state.\n340: In particular:\n341: \n342: - Q0 is a rest identity test. Q1/Q2 require radial inertia, not massless contents.\n343: - Q3 acceleration needs the inertial pressure gradient; constant area does not\n344:   eliminate support work.\n345: - Q4 must distinguish initially rotating contents from a wall rotating around\n346:   initially stationary slip contents. The ellipse also challenges the uniform\n347:   density approximation quantitatively (section 6.2).\n348: - Q5 needs the non-affine solve and its measured cost and validity; a scalar radius\n349:   model cannot claim that case. Q6 must preserve the last valid prefix on failure.\n350: \n351: All content/port solves, geometry and mechanics stage together. Recompute actual\n352: area, density, composition, kinetic energy, circulation consistency, local work,\n353: momentum and support/validity diagnostics before commit. Apply F-contract's prefix\n354: commit semantics and retry caps; no missing interval, negative-amount clipping,\n355: unrecorded circulation change, pressure clamp or silent freeze is permitted.\n356: Attempt logs are diagnostic; physical ledgers change only on accepted steps.\n357: \n358: Checkpoint-relevant content state is chamber/material/port IDs and generations,\n359: species amounts, material constants, circulation and closure version, committed\n360: geometry/motion references, actual time, external/port schedule cursor, and\n361: cumulative physical/numerical accounts. Content momentum represented through the\n362: coupled kinetic closure is stored once in that declared generalized-state\n363: representation; derived P, L and K must agree after restore. Rebuild area/moments,\n364: phi and pi deterministically or save solver/mesh history when it changes the\n",
                                "next_offset": 364,
                                "path": "docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md",
                                "total_lines": 547
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "arguments": {
                                "action": "read",
                                "limit": 30,
                                "offset": 228,
                                "path": "docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md",
                                "text": ""
                            },
                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:db192b4e45aa7000",
                                "lines": "229: ### 5.3 Nullspaces, compatibility and solver\n230: \n231: Build separate systems for every face-connected component. Any component touching\n232: a pressure-Dirichlet face is anchored physically and has no constant nullspace.\n233: Closed, periodic or all-velocity components each have one constant pressure mode;\n234: check their net prescribed flux before removing that mode. A disconnected one-cell\n235: closed component has no free pressure DOF: it still gets the compatibility check.\n236: \n237: For an all-Neumann component, require `abs(sum b)<=1e-12 max(1,sum abs(b))` after\n238: nondimensionalization. A larger imbalance is an incompatible boundary/flux failure,\n239: not a reason to subtract a mean or invent a sink. For accepted roundoff-sized\n240: imbalance only, remove the uniform RHS mean, record that correction, and retain\n241: the original RHS for the final physical residual/volume checks. Pin the smallest\n242: deterministic cell ID to p=0 by removing its row **and column**. Never hide the\n243: pinned row's original residual. Disconnected regions must not share a pin.\n244: \n245: This gauge is a numerical convention. In a sealed resolved internal domain it\n246: does not determine absolute transmembrane pressure or a compressibility law.\n247: The later geometry/mechanics design must supply that physical closure before\n248: coupling deformable walls. An arbitrary gauge must never become a chamber force.\n249: For fixed walls the pressure-gradient/flow solution is meaningful independently\n250: of that missing absolute-pressure closure.\n251: \n252: Use PCG with standard IC(0): deterministic row order; retain only the original\n253: lower-triangle sparsity; compute `L_ii=sqrt(A_ii-sum_k L_ik²)` and retained\n254: `L_ij=(A_ij-sum_k L_ik L_jk)/L_jj`. Apply forward/back substitution for LL^T.\n255: No dropped-fill compensation, diagonal shift, solver fallback or tolerance\n256: relaxation is implicit. Nonpositive/nonfinite pivots or PCG denominators reject\n257: with component/row identity. Build factors for the actual constrained SPD matrix.\n258: Retain pressure as a warm start only after successful commit; tag factors by exact\n",
                                "next_offset": 258,
                                "path": "docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md",
                                "total_lines": 551
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "arguments": {
                                "action": "read",
                                "limit": 35,
                                "offset": 250,
                                "path": "docs/PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md",
                                "text": ""
                            },
                            "output": {
                                "identity": "fnv1a64:577e838481f954a5",
                                "lines": "251: Membranes have zero geometric thickness but retain\n252: explicit side topology at every resolution. Require positive edge lengths ≥0.5 l0\n253: and finite simple non-self-intersecting chamber boundaries. Grid alignment can\n254: produce arbitrarily small cut fragments despite these physical feature limits;\n255: this is not grounds to delete material or relax g_min. Exact zero-measure cuts\n256: may change representation only through an accounted support transition.\n257: \n258: ### 5.2 Required cases and independent oracles\n259: \n260: Each case includes per-owner/local checks as well as global totals. A common\n261: global area or energy sum cannot cancel a local sign/side error. Reset state for\n262: each case; forces generated by prescribed wall motion are external and metered.\n263: \n264: | ID | Construction and forcing | Local oracle | Global/reference oracle |\n265: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n266: | Q0 equilibrium | Base chamber; also repeat at offsets (0,0), (h/2,h/2), (h/3,h/5) and rotations 0, pi/7 | Every velocity, edge strain and pressure difference stays zero within budgets; equal/opposite traction on each facet | No centre drift, net force/torque, area change or unforced energy gain |\n267: | Q1 free expansion | Compliant inflated-inventory chamber, released from rest in exterior fluid | Area-derived pressure, tensile strain, radial velocity field and facet work; record nonradial modes instead of averaging them away | Circular continuum reference below plus polygon refinement; radius extrema, phase, momentum and accounted energy agree |\n268: | Q2 compression/recovery | Prescribed regular polygon radius `R(t)=1-0.05[1-cos(2 pi t/2)]` m; M=rho_ref A0. Period 2 s; R spans 0.9–1. Replace membrane elastic loads by the moving support, retain structure/content inertia | Exact polygon A(t)=A0 R(t)²/R0², p(A), swept volume and local normal velocity; no wall leakage | Over each cycle chamber U returns to initial; integrated support work, open-boundary energy flux and residual close. Compression work on contents is positive |\n269: | Q3 sealed translation | Prescribed base polygon centre `c_x=0.5 sin²(pi t/2)` m, c_y=0, period 2 s; orientation fixed; starts at rest | Zero relative wall-normal flux; constant content amounts/area; adjacent fluid and wall motion remain compatible | Net fluid volume constant, content translation inertia M v_c, paired total impulse/support work. Drag/flow values compared under refinement, not asserted zero |\n270: | Q4 sealed rotation | First prescribed base polygon, then equal-area ellipse sampled with semiaxes a=1.25 m, b=0.8 m; `theta=(pi/2)[1-cos(2 pi t/2)]`, period 2 s | Exact rigid polygon area, rigid content angular inertia from area moments; side preservation at rotating corners | Circular continuum/free-slip limit has no exterior flow from purely tangential wall motion; finite polygon departures must converge. Ellipse pressure torque/work is not assumed zero; compare refinements |\n271: | Q5 narrowing/support | Add two impermeable capsule-shaped supports and the prestressed chamber defined below, initially at rest. `g(t)=0.375+0.125 cos(2 pi t/2)` m, period 2 s; minimum 0.25 m. Translate supports in y and account their external work | Fluid can flow around ends; monitor both gaps, fragment areas, connectivity, swept-area fluxes, pressure extrema and neighbouring side inventories | No artificial force/work/inventory jump when facets cross grid faces/vertices; compare integrated reactions and flow fields under grid/phase refinement. Keep chamber compliant; actual deformed clearance, not nominal g, determines support validity |\n272: | Q6 bounded failure | Q5 supports with `g(t)=0.5-0.3 sin²(pi t/4)` m for 0≤t≤2 s, chamber shape fixed; separately force pressure-solve failure on a chosen attempt and supply invalid nonfinite input | Reject unsupported g<0.25 before commit; rejected candidate changes no committed physical state, RNG or accounts | Actual time/prefix and reason match the last successful substep; retry/iteration limits hold; no fabricated success, disappearing wall or silent frozen motion |\n273: \n274: Q5's supports are externally prescribed rigid solids: all points within distance\n275: r_cap=0.125 m of the horizontal segment from (-2,y_c) to (2,y_c), with\n276: `y_c=±(1+g+r_cap)`. Their nearest surfaces are at y=±(1+g), giving the stated\n277: nominal gaps. Each solid excludes area `8 r_cap + pi r_cap²` from the fluid.\n278: Their geometry belongs to the mechanical supports; exterior actuators absorb\n279: reaction impulse/work, with no finite support inventory inside the world.\n280: Use the same capsule geometry for collision, fluid cuts and swept-area mapping.\n281: If unsupported, report that result; do not substitute a different fixture.\n282: \n283: Q5 starts in a tensile equilibrium rather than an unstressed ring subject to\n284: immediate external compression. Keep the R0 polygon, set rest edge length\n285: `l_rest=l0/1.1`, S=1 N and structural mass `lambda N l_rest`; the initial tension\n",
                                "next_offset": 285,
                                "path": "docs/PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md",
                                "total_lines": 515
                            }
                        }
