        "T-design-boundary": [
            {
                "answer": "The concern is that correct boundary geometry alone will not make the proposed simulations accurate or physically valid. The design identifies three separate limits:\n\n1. Insufficient channel resolution. The 0.25 m no-slip passage is only four cells wide at the proposed grid spacing. The specification predicts 12.5% flow error against a proposed 2% limit. This needs a resolution or numerical-method study—not just better geometry bookkeeping. It is not a measured failure of the free-slip Q5 case.\n\n2. A chamber model outside its validity range. Q4’s rotating interior predicts fluid speed at about 0.817 times the model’s sound speed, versus its proposed 0.1 limit. Conserving displaced volume and pressure work cannot fix that mismatch. A different physical model or a reviewed change to the test conditions is needed.\n\n3. Missing physics for sealed, volume-changing interiors. An incompressible fluid cannot accommodate a sealed container changing volume. The stationary flow law also does not supply the absolute pressure needed for wall loads. This requires an explicit physical closure, such as appropriate compressibility; switching to resolved fluid is not itself a solution.\n\nThese are serious blockers for qualifying the affected cases, not proof that the boundary-design task itself failed. docs/BOUNDARY_LAW.md explicitly records these limits and says its parameters remain unapproved; the worker’s checks establish specification arithmetic, not working simulation performance.\n\nThe hold remains because review-0166791539692af38abce208ac23624d failed or was interrupted and has no resolution. The next step is to resume that review and decide which numerical/model changes or scope changes to propose before affected qualification proceeds.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-10 00:50 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-d8ee77302d68936f770135e1ec72f653",
                "note": "",
