            {
                "answer": "Yes—these could block accurate simulations, but they are design-stage problems, not evidence that the whole architecture needs replacing. There are potentially inexpensive options; none has demonstrated performance yet.\n\nI would recommend a targeted approach:\n\n1. Keep the reduced chamber model wherever it is valid. It already includes compressibility and pressure-storage energy in docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md. That avoids automatically replacing every chamber with a resolved fluid grid. It does not, however, fix Q4’s excessive internal speed.\n\n2. Decide whether Q4’s current speed is actually required. Reducing its rotation rate by roughly 8.2 times would address the reported speed/sound-speed screen, with the other parameters fixed. That changes the experiment, not the solver’s accuracy, and would need approval plus rechecking of the remaining requirements. If that speed is essential, a more capable interior model is needed; I cannot promise a negligible cost. Increasing stiffness instead may make time integration more expensive.\n\n3. Investigate a better no-slip wall discretization before refining the entire grid. This is a plausible way to reduce the narrow-channel error without greatly increasing the number of unknowns. It must demonstrate accurate flow, wall forces and energy accounting—not merely match one channel example. Global refinement is the simpler fallback, but potentially costly; adaptive refinement is explicitly deferred in the current architecture.\n\nFor sealed interiors that change volume, keep an explicit compressible physical model. Switching them to the current incompressible grid solver would not solve the incompatibility.\n\nThe practical next step is to have “Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification” compare these targeted options, retaining the accuracy limits and requiring equal-accuracy cost measurements before adoption. No significant-performance-impact guarantee is available from the current specification arithmetic.\n\nOne correction to my earlier answer: the current review, review-0166791539692af38abce208ac23624d, is completed and kept the boundary design. It directs these compatibility questions to reconciliation before implementation approval; it is not an interrupted review needing resumption.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-10 13:34 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-fdafea42b63536d5e02c68e8616e91b2",
                "note": "",
                "plan_revision": 4,
