            {
                "answer": "You shouldn’t have to choose a speed from a solver equation. You should choose how quickly organisms need to move and deform; we then need to check whether the model can represent that reliably. My earlier suggestion made that sound too much like your responsibility.\n\nAlso, “speed” here means physical motion inside the simulated world—not frame rate or how quickly the program runs.\n\nThe concrete example is the sealed-rotation test, labelled Q4 in docs/PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md: an oval chamber turns through 180° and back over two simulated seconds. Slowing it by roughly 8.2 times would make that same cycle take about 16.4 seconds. Visually, that is a quick turn becoming a leisurely movement. It is not simply a performance optimization: fluid velocities and dynamic forces also change.\n\nKeeping the current physical speed with the proposed simplified interior model is a different issue. Pressure disturbances take time to cross the chamber, but this model is intended for conditions where that delay is relatively small. At the specified speed, that assumption is outside its proposed validity limits.\n\nWhat would look wrong? Potentially the timing and strength of fluid motion and pressure forces. In a freely deforming organism, those errors could affect wobbling, deformation or recoil. But in this particular test the oval’s motion is prescribed, so its outline would still turn exactly as commanded—even if the calculated forces were inaccurate. A convincing animation would not establish correctness.\n\nWe do not yet have a comparison showing actual visual errors. docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md, section 6.2, explicitly describes an assumption mismatch, not a measured simulation failure. It also does not establish that dramatic effects such as shocks would appear.\n\nThe useful decision is therefore: do you want organisms of this size to make movements this rapid, or would much slower movement meet the vision? You can answer with an example animation or “turn its body in about a second”; you don’t need to specify a sound-speed ratio.\n\nMy recommendation is to keep the current test unchanged for now and propose a comparison against a suitable reference model at representative organism speeds. That would show whether the difference matters visually and mechanically before asking you to sacrifice motion or accept a more expensive model.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-10 13:48 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-b10ddef0eb45d11fe9a7a1dbb6348b92",
                "note": "",
                "plan_revision": 4,
                "question": "help my understand your second point. How am I supposed to know if that speed is essential? What would be visually different with the current speed in the resulting simulation?",
