            {
                "answer": "Yes—you may be able to get away with the cheaper approximation for “pretty good” organisms. I was too categorical earlier: exceeding a proposed validity limit is a warning, not proof of a visible problem. It does not establish that you need slower fish or an expensive replacement.\n\nBut the possible effects are behavioural, not just decimal-place accuracy:\n\nIf a fish swims by flexing its body and pushing against water, errors in fluid forces could change its swimming speed, turning, recoil or body deformation for the same muscle effort. If its position is simply animated, it could look identical despite incorrect forces.\n\nEnvironmental reactions could also change if its controller responds to simulated pressure, flow or deformation. That does not mean its decision-making code would be wrong; it might receive different physical inputs. The internal-chamber approximation is not, by itself, evidence that all external-water interactions would be inaccurate.\n\nWe cannot currently say how noticeable any of this would be. The cited 12.5% passage-flow estimate is not a prediction of 12.5% swimming-speed error. And the chamber’s speed ratio is a screening metric, not an error percentage. docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md, section 5, explicitly calls these conservative screens rather than error proofs. The inspected Substrate/Substrate.cpp update loop only advances diagnostic counters, so there is no demonstrated swimming behaviour to judge yet.\n\nFor your goal, I would propose that Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification (T-reconcile) define “good enough” using representative swimming, turning and current-response comparisons against a more reliable reference. Compare visible motion, speed, response timing and runtime—not just pressure accuracy. If differences are modest and behaviour stays stable, accepting the approximation and revising the proposed tolerances could be entirely reasonable.\n\nI would still keep safeguards against artificial energy creation, leaking contents or unstable motion: those can become conspicuous over time. But no, I would not ask you to sacrifice lively movement merely to satisfy an unvalidated numerical threshold.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-10 14:52 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-cee2dfc6c3a4fed476202c946482ddce",
                "note": "",
                "plan_revision": 4,
                "question": "but I'm not after perfect correctness, just \"pretty good\". Are you saying I can maybe just get away with it? For example, would this affect how fast a fish could swim or the way it reacts to its environment? Or are you just obsessing over numerical accuracy that won't really be visually noticable?",
