            {
                "answer": "My recommendation: accept this boundary-design task and move on to reconciliation—not to unconditional implementation approval. This is not an emergency requiring a more realistic or expensive physics engine.\n\nYour goal needs consistent, convincing rules, not an exact replica of nature. A custom periodic table and simplified organs are entirely compatible with that. My earlier answers put too much emphasis on numerical thresholds whose visible importance has not been demonstrated.\n\nThe important distinction is between approximate physics and exploitable inconsistencies. Modestly inaccurate drag may be acceptable. Movement that generates free energy, disappearing contents, or unstable organs would undermine the world you want. In an evolutionary simulation, organisms could potentially benefit from those numerical loopholes rather than develop useful adaptations.\n\nThe current review, review-0166791539692af38abce208ac23624d, kept Design sided moving geometry and cut-cell exchanges (T-design-boundary). It treats the concerns as compatibility questions for Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification (T-reconcile), not a failure of this task. The inspected docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md also explicitly describes a candidate model, not demonstrated dynamics. There is no basis yet for saying your fish would look wrong—or that the approximation is good enough.\n\nI would ask reconciliation to propose:\n\n• Affordable approximations judged against representative motion and deformation, with runtime measured alongside behavioural differences.\n• Firm safeguards against artificial energy, leaking contents and instability, while allowing justified changes to precision targets.\n• A consistent pressure model for changing-volume organs; it need not reproduce real biology perfectly.\n\nThose decisions belong before Approve the bounded numerical work package (T-approve). Expensive model replacement does not need deciding now.\n\nOne broader expectation matters: the current architecture explicitly defers growth, reproduction, evolution and rich-world scale. This work is a foundation experiment for your vision, not delivery of that ecosystem. Accepting this task is reasonable; accepting the entire foundation as proven suitable for evolving organisms would be premature.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-10 14:57 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-ba3739ecb79af955f05e2c68c75da603",
                "note": "",
                "plan_revision": 4,
                "question": "I'm more focused on making an environment where organisms like fish can evolve different body shapes, compete with eachother, plants/vegitation can grows, animals can grow limbs and walk, they can develop internal organs, etc. I'm not trying to perfectly model reality, but I want the environment to \"feel\" real, complex and rich by giving it good enough physics and my own custom \"periodic table\".\n\nWith that in mind, I want to know whether I just approve and move on, or whether this is truly urgent to deal with right now.",
