            },
            {
                "answer": "Imagine one of your creatures as a water-balloon animal squeezing through a narrow gap between two rocks.\n\nWith a convincing response, its front squashes against the gap, the body bulges, and the back follows with some lag. It looks like a heavy, fluid-filled creature pushing itself through.\n\nA possible “weird” version: the front hits the rock and the back reacts too promptly, as though the contents transmit the shove instantly. Instead of a soft bag with weight inside, it looks more like a rubber prop whose whole shape moves together. During rapid wriggling, its recoil could also be stronger or weaker than you’d expect.\n\nThat’s an illustration of the concern, not an observed failure. The proposed model in docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md includes real mass and inertia, but leaves out travelling pressure waves. It could look perfectly fine during slower movement.\n\nFor your goal, the important distinction is cosmetic weirdness versus consequential weirdness. Missing a little wobble is probably fine. Getting the resistance or recoil wrong enough that rapid-wriggling creatures gain an artificial advantage would matter: the simplification could shape which creatures thrive.\n\nI’d propose a simple visual test of that balloon-like creature squeezing and wriggling, slowly and quickly, alongside a more detailed reference. Compare both the animation and the effort needed to move. You don’t need perfect physics—you need the shortcut not to undermine the interactions that make the world interesting.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-09 23:57 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-30c8b5bdcb2c7495497601e15c5b4825",
                "note": "",
                "plan_revision": 4,
                "question": "my end goal doesn't really require \"perfect\" looking physics, but it does need to be reliable and support the goal of producing a nice rich complex environment. Can you give me a visual example of how this result could look \"weird\" in the final result? Use an example that's relatable",
                "receipt": {
