                "status": "completed",
                "target_revision": 0
            },
            {
                "answer": "Together, they could make a creature look oddly effective at rapid squeezing: its body reacts too much as one piece, while surrounding liquid escapes through tight passages too readily. That could matter for your rich environment—not just its appearance—if it rewards the wrong movement strategies. But there is no measured combined failure yet, or meaningful percentage we can add together.\n\nImagine a water-filled rubber toy wriggling through a flooded drainpipe, leaving only narrow gaps beside it.\n\nA convincing version would squash, push water through those gaps, and advance with resistance and some delayed internal response.\n\nThe potentially weird version would look like a rubber prop sliding through an unusually slippery pipe. Each quick squeeze makes its front and back react too promptly together, while water streams past the sides more easily than it should. It might appear to “scoot” through a tight spot where you expect a slower, heavier struggle.\n\nThe two concerns contribute differently:\n\nThe compartment shortcut leaves out travelling pressure waves. During fast motion, that can distort the timing and strength of wall forces. We do not yet know whether it would help or hinder this particular creature.\n\nThe stationary-flow design predicts 12.5% too much flow for the same driving force in its specific four-cell-wide, viscous no-slip channel (docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md, section R3). That supports the overly-easy-drainage part of the analogy, but is not a measured error for a moving creature or every narrow gap.\n\nSo this is serious enough to resolve before trusting which creatures perform best, but not evidence that the overall approach is broken. Accurate conservation alone would not prevent it: the energy accounts could balance while resistance and response timing are wrong. Both reviews kept the design work while requiring reconciliation before implementation approval.\n\nA useful proposed test is that rubber-toy scene, with slow and fast squeezing, compared against a suitable interior reference and better-resolved surrounding gaps—changing each separately, then both. Compare progress per unit effort as well as animation. If correcting the shortcuts changes which movement strategy wins, that is the consequential weirdness to fix.",
