            {
                "answer": "For the narrow-gap flow problem, largely yes. For the compartment problem, no—not just by making the grid finer. Neither demonstrates that your overall project is unworkable.\n\nThe flow issue is a resolution problem: too few cells across a narrow gap. In docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md, section R3, increasing from 4 to 16 cells across the same gap reduces the predicted flow error from 12.5% to about 0.78%, meeting both proposed accuracy gates for that reference. That is a genuine performance-versus-accuracy trade-off, although runtime cost and moving-creature accuracy remain unmeasured.\n\nThe compartment issue is a model limitation: it deliberately leaves out travelling pressure waves. Smaller cells or shorter timesteps cannot restore physics the model does not represent. Think of sharpening an image versus adding something missing from the picture.\n\nThere are two possible routes for that:\n\nUse a more detailed interior model that represents those effects. This could ultimately become a performance-versus-quality choice, but requires model/solver work and validation—not merely changing a few values.\n\nKeep the simplified model and restrict the physical conditions to where it works reliably—for example, slower creature motions. That changes what the simulated world supports, rather than just making the computer take longer to simulate the same world.\n\nSo these are blockers to approving the current proposals unchanged, not fundamental blockers to your goal. The practical next step is to establish whether your intended creature behaviours fit the simpler compartment model, while checking the cost of sufficient gap resolution. You don’t need perfect physics; you do need evidence that the affordable approximation preserves the interactions you care about.",
