            {
                "answer": "I’d give them attention now, but not launch a major redesign. Decide how they will be resolved before implementation approval; defer expensive improvements until targeted tests show they’re needed.\n\nThe distinction is how costly a later correction could be:\n\nThe narrow-gap flow concern is the more deferrable one. Finer resolution is a plausible remedy, but you still need to establish whether sufficient accuracy is affordable. Otherwise you could build a world whose important passages are too expensive to simulate reliably.\n\nThe compartment concern deserves an earlier decision. In docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md, simplified contents and resolved interior fluid have different state ownership. Replacing one with the other could therefore affect coupling, saved state and validation—not just a quality setting. That doesn’t mean replacing the whole engine, but I wouldn’t assume it will be a cheap late fix.\n\nTogether, the main risk is building creature behaviours around inaccurate resistance and recoil, then discovering that correcting the physics changes which behaviours succeed. There is still no measured combined failure, so this is a reason for an early check, not evidence that the project needs abandoning.\n\nMy recommendation is to use the existing “Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification” task to settle the supported motion range, interior-model choice and gap-resolution strategy. Both current reviews already require reconciliation before implementation approval.\n\nThen propose a small qualification experiment before investing in rich creature behaviour: compare slow and fast squeezing through a narrow passage, varying interior treatment and gap resolution separately. Check effort and movement effectiveness, not just appearance.\n\nIn short: you can defer the higher-fidelity solution, but shouldn’t defer deciding whether the simpler solution supports the world you want. No need to perfect everything now; avoid making the expensive parts depend on an unchecked assumption.",
