                "answer": "Yes—with one important distinction: use “Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification” as a decision gate, not just a place to note the concerns and carry on.\n\nContinue the design work up to that point. At reconciliation, require a clear answer to whether the proposed interior model and narrow-gap resolution can support the creature behaviours you want, and what evidence is needed to establish that.\n\nThat task may settle the design choice without proving the final behaviour. If targeted experiments are still needed, they should be explicit prerequisites for trusting the affected physics—not left until after substantial creature development.\n\nSo: proceed to reconciliation, let it determine the resolution and validation path, and don’t approve implementation on the assumption that both concerns will be cheap fixes later. Neither currently proves your overall approach is broken.",
