            {
                "answer": "For the selected stationary-flow design, the main concern is accuracy at the planned minimum passage size—not failure to finish the design task.\n\nAt a 0.25 m passage width and 1/16 m grid spacing, the passage is only four cells wide. The proposed no-slip method predicts 12.5% flow/work error and 6.25% pointwise velocity error, exceeding the proposed limits of 2% and 0.5%. This is documented in docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md.\n\nThat is a serious adoption issue: the configuration cannot satisfy its own proposed accuracy gates. Review review-78da2ac08c3a91845f1d7b35453eb922 kept the design task completed but requires “Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification” to resolve the incompatibility before implementation approval. No finer grid or relaxed budget has been approved.\n\nThe practical trade-off is accuracy versus cost. A finer grid or revised boundary treatment would need accuracy evidence and complete-work performance measurements—not just successful arithmetic checks.\n\nMoving-boundary compatibility, implemented solver behavior and runtime performance also remain unproven. Those are downstream validation needs, not failures of this stationary-only task. The no-slip result does not itself invalidate the contract’s inviscid, free-slip fixtures.\n\nThis is the confirmed concern for the selected task, rather than an exhaustive audit of the whole plan.",
