            {
                "answer": "Keep progressing with the planned design and review steps—yes. Assume the finished simulation will work without changes—no. There isn’t enough evidence to give an honest percentage.\n\nELI5: you’ve checked that the workshop’s lights work and drawn some blueprints. One blueprint already shows that a pipe won’t meet your accuracy target. That’s useful early discovery, not a ruined project.\n\nYour completed work tells us:\n\n• The diagnostic pilot worked for its limited purpose, and review accepted it. You also approved continuing through the saved-review check. This proves basic workflow, not working physics; Substrate/Substrate.cpp currently advances diagnostic counters.\n• The shared physical contract is completed as a specification. Its accuracy and performance targets are still proposals, not demonstrated results.\n• The stationary-flow design found a definite mismatch: in the narrow passage, its formula predicts 12.5% flow error against a proposed 2% limit. That’s not merely a possible future problem, but it is a design calculation—not an observed solver failure.\n\nThe practical risk is rework. Ignoring that mismatch and building around it could make later changes expensive. Resolving it now could mean changing the grid, boundary method or supported requirements; the cost and accuracy trade-off still needs evidence.\n\nFortunately, your plan already includes the right stopping point: “Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification,” followed by your approval before implementation. The current review explicitly requires resolving this mismatch there.\n\nMy recommendation: continue the remaining design tasks, but don’t wave this concern through at approval. Nothing reviewed establishes that the whole plan is doomed; equally, the completed tasks are too early to establish that the eventual physics will be accurate or fast.",
