            {
                "answer": "Yes—the project could still run, but this particular configuration would predict narrow-passage flow inaccurately. It is not evidence that the whole project cannot work.\n\nWhy it misses 2%: the proposed grid gives the narrowest passage only four cells across. The chosen approximation of fluid sticking to the walls makes the calculated velocity too high. Even solving those grid equations perfectly would not remove that error—it is an approximation error, not insufficient solver effort.\n\nSection 7 of docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md derives the flow error as 2 ÷ (cells across)². With four cells, that is 12.5%, rather than the proposed maximum of 2%.\n\nThe practical meaning in this reference case:\n• For the same driving pressure gradient, the predicted flow is 12.5% too high: think 112.5 units passing through where the physical answer is 100.\n• The corresponding steady driving power is also 12.5% too high.\n• Local velocity has an error equal to 6.25% of the true peak speed—not necessarily 6.25% at every location.\n\nIf later behavior depends on that flow, delivery rates and transport times could consequently be wrong. That is a potential downstream consequence, not a measured result yet. Nor does it mean every quantity is wrong: the derived wall shear in this reference remains exact.\n\nSo this is serious for trustworthy quantitative physics, but it does not by itself establish instability, a crash, or failure of unrelated flow cases. These numbers are design calculations, not results from a running fluid solver.\n\nThe proposed remedies are a finer grid, a better wall approximation, or explicitly revised requirements. For this reference, 10 cells across would meet the 2% flow limit, but at least 15 are needed for the separate 0.5% velocity limit. Finer grids cost more; actual accuracy and runtime would still need testing. The existing reconciliation task must resolve that trade-off before implementation approval.",
