            {
                "answer": "“Narrow-passage flow” means fluid squeezing through a small gap—like water between two closely spaced plates. In this design, the smallest passage is 25 cm wide but represented by only four grid cells across. That gives the simulation very little detail to capture how fluid sticks to the walls and moves faster through the middle.\n\nThe percentages measure how much fluid passes through, not how “wrong the picture” looks. In this particular reference case, under the same driving pressure:\n• Real flow: 100 litres per second.\n• Predicted flow with this 12.5% overestimate: 112.5 litres per second.\n• A 2% error limit would allow 98–102 litres per second.\n\nThere’s no magic boundary at 2%: it’s the proposed accuracy requirement. A 12.5% error is 6.25 times that allowance.\n\nVisually, both could look smooth and believable. If you watched dye or particles travelling through the gap, the inaccurate flow could carry them through too quickly. If that passage fed a tank, it could fill too quickly. You would more easily notice the difference in a timed side-by-side comparison than in a single image.\n\nIt does not mean 12.5% more splashing, visibly broken fluid, or every particle moving 12.5% faster. Total flow error and local speed error are different measurements.\n\nThe lesson: a simulation can look convincing while getting transport rates wrong. These numbers are design predictions, not observed animation results; the actual visual difference still needs an implemented test.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-09 23:22 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-d546425e9de8abb1d74a2e60d93169c8",
                "note": "",
                "plan_revision": 4,
                "question": "I need a short lesson here. What is \"narrow passage\" flow? What does a 12.5% error mean that a 2% error doesn't? What would be the difference visually in the resulting fluid?",
                "receipt": {
