            {
                "answer": "Not simply, no. The issue may be redeemable, but bigger organisms and smaller timesteps address different things—and bigger organisms can actually worsen the physical limitation.\n\nA smaller timestep means calculating the same movement in more increments. It can improve numerical accuracy, but it does not make the organism’s acceleration gentler or let pressure travel through its body faster.\n\nA bigger body gives pressure farther to travel. With the same material and movement period, that makes the uniform-density approximation harder to justify. At the same acceleration, it also requires larger internal pressure differences. These relationships are explicit in docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md, section 5.\n\nWhat could help is slower physical movement—not just smaller computational steps. For example, the translation test in docs/PASSIVE_SPEC.md moves a body of radius 1 metre through a 2-second cycle. Keeping its size and displacement unchanged but stretching that cycle to about 10 seconds would analytically bring its reported acceleration-pressure and acoustic checks within the proposed limits. That is a promising adjustment, not a demonstrated recovery of the whole package.\n\nThere is one place where bigger helps: wider passages contain more grid cells at the same grid spacing, which can improve flow accuracy. But merely drawing organisms larger on screen changes neither problem.\n\nSo the practical answer is: slower, gentler organisms may fit the existing model; larger organisms moving just as briskly generally will not. If fast squeezing and internal pressure waves are essential, timestep reduction cannot supply the missing physics. The next useful proposal should quantify acceptable sizes and movement periods across complete cycles, so you can judge whether the resulting behaviour is lively enough before committing to this design.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-10 15:13 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-a94cac7b30032f02a43e6bf91b713ae5",
                "note": "",
                "plan_revision": 4,
                "question": "is the issue redeemable by simply making my organisms bigger and reducing my timestep?",
