            {
                "answer": "My fix would be to keep inexpensive compartments for ordinary organs, and add spatially resolved, compressible interiors only where their behaviour actually requires them. I would not make the entire world a high-resolution compressible fluid simulation—or make every organism sluggish just to fit the current approximation.\n\nThere are two separate problems to address.\n\n1. Separate soft bodies from easily compressed contents.\n\nA soft, water-filled bag changes shape readily without its liquid substantially changing volume. Your organisms could likewise get much of their softness from membranes, tissue and movement between chambers—not from highly compressible liquid.\n\nI would first investigate a stiffer liquid material while preserving flexible structures and real transfer paths. That could make the inexpensive chamber approximation valid for brisker movement. But this changes the physics: squeezing a sealed chamber to a smaller volume would require more force and energy. It cannot be a hidden numerical adjustment, and it would not make every existing compression test pass unchanged.\n\nThe pressure, energy and sound-speed relationships making this tradeoff explicit are in docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md, section 2. We should evaluate movement, pumping and deformation together, rather than tune material stiffness to pass translation alone.\n\n2. Supply the missing interior model where compression and internal flow matter.\n\nFor a chamber that genuinely needs rapid compression, pressure propagation or spatial nutrient transport, I would propose a conservative compressible interior model: local mass and momentum, pressure derived from density, and pressure work exchanged with the moving membrane.\n\nThe performance strategy would be to restrict that expensive representation to selected chambers, chosen when constructing the organism. Ordinary storage compartments would retain the cheaper model; the exterior would retain its existing incompressible approach where appropriate. No automatic switching in the first version, and never two representations owning the same contents.\n\nI would investigate implicit treatment of acoustic pressure so fast sound propagation need not automatically force tiny explicit timesteps. That is a candidate numerical design, not a guaranteed speedup: its coupled solves may still be expensive, and accurately depicting a pressure wave still requires time resolution. The current incompressible solver cannot simply be reused unchanged for this purpose; docs/PASSIVE_SPEC.md, lines 115–120, identifies the missing closure.\n\nFor narrow passages, I would tackle accuracy separately. The audited no-slip channel predicts acceptable errors at 16 cells across its width, versus four currently. Applying that fourfold refinement everywhere in 2D means roughly 16 times as many cells, before timestep and solver costs. I would establish a trustworthy fine reference first, then assess a more accurate conservative wall discretization for a cheaper production configuration. Neither improvement is yet demonstrated.\n\nBefore committing, I would compare the alternatives on complete squeezing, pumping and movement cycles, measuring both physical error and total cost per simulated second—including geometry, coupling, retries and staging. Even the existing compartment model needs auxiliary solves; ‘reduced’ does not mean free.\n\nMy recommendation is therefore selective physical detail, not globally smaller timesteps. Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification (T-reconcile) can quantify the existing-law option. A new compressible interior needs a separately approved design extension. I would not call either route recovered until those accuracy-and-cost comparisons exist.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-10 15:16 UTC",
                "error": "",
                "id": "chat-753c70df96e1aecf281609fd29a85a19",
                "note": "",
