        {
            "execution_attempt": "5cb403c1-01d2-4cbb-8e7f-7806f208d138",
            "id": "task-request-dbfd24f168633e91db7bf782c57dd585",
            "input": "MindGraph task T-reconcile: Reconcile the coupled advancement and experiment specification\n\nProduce an implementable passive work package and predeclared gates.\n\nReconcile existing mechanism designs rather than redesigning the whole engine. Choose substep ordering, two-way solve convergence and staging ownership; reserve finite transfer and actuator interfaces.\n\nDone when:\n- F-passive-spec resolves all units, signs, duplicated mass risks, geometry timing, boundary conditions, pressure/force exchange and rollback across mechanisms.\n- Freeze proposed passive configurations, local and global tolerances, refinement comparisons, quality-matched full-cost rejection budgets and horizon rationale.\n- Specify failure injection and low-frequency passive-motion tests that detect gain, excess damping, leakage and collapsed features.\n- Identify expected implementation paths and test locations without adding speculative subsystem frameworks.\n\nInput F-contract (Shared physical and state contract): Located specification of owners, units, interfaces, state lifecycle, passive fixtures and proposed budgets.\n\nInput F-mechanics-law (Mechanics algorithm and reference cases): Implementable constitutive/contact law, solver, failure rules and worked mechanical oracles.\n\nInput F-contents-law (Compartment algorithm and reference cases): Geometry-based pressure, mass/inertia, energy/work and transfer contract with validity limits.\n\nInput F-flow-law (Stationary flow algorithm and reference cases): Control-volume layout, flux/pressure algorithm, boundary conditions, convergence and quantitative references.\n\nInput F-boundary-law (Moving-boundary algorithm and reference cases): Sided cut geometry, small-cell treatment, swept volumes, traction/work exchanges and support-transition oracles.\n\nOutput F-passive-spec (Reconciled passive implementation package): Complete coupled schedule, authoritative exchanges, staging/failure design and predeclared qualification configurations/budgets.\n\nContract C-world: Own stable structural/material references, authoritative committed state, units, advancement schedule, bounded retries and validity checks. Preserve the last valid state and actual time on failure.\n\nContract C-boundary: Derive cut geometry and sided barriers from authoritative structures; reconcile swept volumes, fluxes, pressure forces, work and support transitions without owning duplicate inventory.\n\nHandoff from T-contract:\nThe shared contract is complete and rechecked. F-contract is docs/PHYSICAL_CONTRACT.md, draft 1.1. Preserved the existing specification, corrected mass-fraction and force/impulse wording, and defined the refinement floor. It specifies ownership, units, exchanges, failure/restart semantics, passive fixtures, proposed budgets and complete-work boundaries. Relevant capability guidance used: bitloop, simulation-health and snapshots-checkpoints. Numerical proposals remain unapproved; these checks validate the specification and arithmetic, not physical behavior or performance.\n- Reported passed: Inspected AGENTS.md, relevant Bitloop guidance, current source and existing F-contract. Reviewed all four acceptance criteria: owners/interfaces/state, fixtures, proposed budgets/refinement, and complete-work timing/memory boundaries are specified.\n- Reported passed: PowerShell document checks passed: 15 required terms, seven fixtures Q0-Q6, five valid local links, explicit unapproved-value labelling, and removal of incorrect dimensional wording.\n- Reported passed: Reran arithmetic checks: pressure/energy identity error 1.12e-10 N/m below 1e-8; radial energy identity residual -7.71e-17 W below 1e-12; prestress force residual zero; Q6 clearance reaches 0.25 m at 1.4645590544 s; 496 subdivision attempts fit the proposed 512 cap. Evidence is documented in F-contract section 10.\n- Reported passed: git diff --check and document whitespace checks passed. Get-FileHash comparisons confirmed both Substrate source files are unchanged from the retained pilot source identities.\n\nHandoff from T-design-mechanics:\nSpecified one mechanics candidate with tissue triangles, tensile membrane/fiber elements, projected implicit midpoint, bounded contact/friction solves and inversion prevention. F-mechanics-law is docs/MECHANICS_LAW.md; reproducible reference calculations are tooling/check_mechanics_law.ps1, with conditions and decisive findings retained in sections 5–7. Defines mass, reactions, work, failure, resolution and fuel-limited actuator interfaces. Used capabilities bitloop and simulation-health. Checks establish specification arithmetic, not implemented dynamics or performance. Dry contact references do not qualify wet contact below the contract's 0.25 m fluid-gap limit; numerical parameters remain unapproved.\n- Reported passed: Inspected F-contract draft 1.1, AGENTS.md, relevant local guidance and current source; reviewed all three acceptance criteria against MECHANICS_LAW.md sections 1–6.\n- Reported passed: ./tooling/check_mechanics_law.ps1 -Document: 33 binary64 algebra checks, three negative controls and document/link checks passed. Force-gradient error 5.95e-11 N versus 1e-8 tolerance; compression-work error 1.41e-11 J versus 1e-8 tolerance.\n- Reported passed: git diff --check, explicit whitespace checks and Get-FileHash comparisons passed; existing source, CMake, dependency manifest, F-contract and other design artifacts remain unchanged.\n\nHandoff from T-design-contents:\nDesigned pressure-bearing compartments with real inertia and conservative transfer accounts. F-contents-law is docs/COMPARTMENT_LAW.md; reproducible checks are tooling/check_contents_law.ps1, with decisive observations retained in sections 6–7. Specifies actual-area/inventory pressure, stored energy, independent circulation, inertial wall loads, paired work, finite transfers, validity and restart semantics. Used capabilities bitloop and simulation-health. Numerical proposals remain unapproved. Checks establish specification arithmetic, not physical behavior, performance or coupled opening-flow validity.\n- Reported passed: Inspected F-contract draft 1.1 and current source; reviewed all three acceptance criteria against COMPARTMENT_LAW.md sections 1–6.\n- Reported passed: ./tooling/check_contents_law.ps1 -Document: 20 binary64 algebra checks plus document/link checks passed. Pressure derivative error 1.12e-10 N/m versus 1e-8 tolerance; finite-transfer energy residual 1.66e-19 J versus 1e-12 tolerance.\n- Reported passed: git diff --check, explicit whitespace checks and Get-FileHash comparisons passed; existing source, CMake, dependencies, F-contract and flow specification remain unchanged.\n- Reported plan concern: The existing Q4 ellipse at its 2 s period implies an irrotational interior speed of 2.58363 m/s, giving speed/c=0.817 versus this reduction's proposed 0.1 validity screen. A suitable resolved interior or explicitly approved scale change is needed before qualification; the fixture was preserved.\n\nHandoff from T-design-flow:\nSpecified one stationary-flow candidate and its quantitative limitations. F-flow-law is docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md: conservative staggered control volumes, backward-Euler symmetric viscous stress, PCG/IC(0) pressure projection, component nullspaces, boundary conditions, bounded failure, worked references and complete-work instrumentation. Relevant capability guidance used: bitloop and simulation-health. Section 10 retains arithmetic evidence. Numerical values remain unapproved; algebra checks do not establish implemented solver behavior, moving-boundary compatibility or runtime performance.\n- Reported passed: Inspected F-contract draft 1.1, AGENTS.md, relevant Bitloop guidance and current source. Reviewed all three acceptance groups in docs/STATIONARY_FLOW_LAW.md: state/algorithm/failure rules, worked references, and disconnected-region/narrow-passage instrumentation.\n- Reported passed: PowerShell algebra checks passed: channel stencil residual 7.08e-16 N/m² below 1e-12; discrete driving power and viscous dissipation both 0.016796875 W; viscous-decay energy identity error 1.17e-18 J below 1e-14; two-cell pressure projection produced zero divergence and the expected 0.5 J numerical energy loss. Evidence: section 10.\n- Reported passed: Recalculated channel and decay references at 16/32/64 cells. Confirmed the four-cell minimum-gap channel predicts 12.5% flow/work error, exceeding the proposed 2% gate. This negative prediction is explicitly retained in sections 1, 7 and 10.\n- Reported passed: Document coverage, local-link and whitespace checks passed; git diff --check passed. Hash comparisons confirmed F-contract and six source/build/dependency configuration files were unchanged.\n- Reported plan concern: The selected no-slip stencil predicts 12.5% flow/work error and 6.25% pointwise velocity error in F-contract's 0.25 m passage at h=1/16 m. These exceed the proposed stationary gates of 2% and 0.5%. Adoption at that resolution requires review; no grid or budget change was applied.\n\nHandoff from T-design-boundary:\nSpecified sided moving geometry, conservative small-cell treatment, swept inventories, traction/work pairing, continuity tests and bounded failure. F-boundary-law: C:/dev/cpp/bitloop-workspace/bitloop-gallery/projects/Substrate/docs/BOUNDARY_LAW.md. Reproducible arithmetic: C:/dev/cpp/bitloop-workspace/bitloop-gallery/projects/Substrate/tooling/check_boundary_law.ps1; decisive findings and input identities are retained in specification section 9. Used capabilities bitloop and simulation-health. Parameters remain unapproved; these checks establish specification arithmetic, not implemented dynamics, coupled qualification or performance. No runtime or MindGraph routing code changed.\n- Reported passed: Inspected all four input specifications, project instructions, relevant skill guidance and current source; reviewed boundary-law sections 1–7 against all acceptance criteria.\n- Reported passed: ./tooling/check_boundary_law.ps1 -Document: 43 binary64 arithmetic checks and five negative-control groups passed. Polygon sweep error 6.76e-15 m² and radial energy residual -9.44e-17 W were below 1e-12 arithmetic tolerances. Document coverage, links, whitespace and input/source hashes passed.\n- Reported passed: git diff --check passed. Get-FileHash comparisons confirmed all four input specifications and six source/build/dependency files remain unchanged.\n- Reported plan concern: Existing numerical limits remain: the 0.25 m no-slip passage at h=1/16 m predicts 12.5% flow error versus the proposed 2% gate; Q4's reduced interior predicts speed/c=0.817 versus 0.1. The stationary incompressible law also cannot support sealed volume-changing interiors without an additional physical closure. These require review before affected qualification.\n\nFollow the project's AGENTS.md and relevant local skills. Execute only this task; do not delegate. Inspect the required inputs and relevant source. Artifact descriptions specify expected deliverables, not proof they exist. If a required input or decision is missing, report the blocker instead of expanding scope.\n\nEnd your reply with one fenced JSON block labelled mindgraph-result, with nothing after it:\n{\"checks\":[{\"name\":\"Required check: command and observed result\",\"passed\":true}],\"issues\":[],\"plan_concern\":null,\"status\":\"succeeded\",\"summary\":\"Plain-language outcome first; output artifact IDs, paths and usable guarantees; only relevant limitations\",\"task_id\":\"T-reconcile\"}\nUse succeeded when this task's criteria are met and all required checks passed; issues for incomplete work or blocked/failed required checks; failed for no usable result. Never invent checks. Record only this task's required checks in checks; put out-of-scope or deferred validation in summary, not as failures. Success requires at least one passing check and no issues. Keep the report concise but retain decisive observations and evidence paths. For experiments, include measurements, conditions and threshold sources. Set plan_concern only for actual evidence challenging a relied-on design or requirement; an untested claim outside this task's scope is not a concern. Completing a negative experiment does not authorize design adoption.\n",
            "input_basis": "c1d1f507d331f874",
            "plan_revision": 4,
            "project_capabilities": {
                "catalogue": [
                    {
                        "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/bitloop.md",
                        "id": "bitloop",
                        "summary": "# Bitloop project and skill routing\n\nActions: use the existing Bitloop lifecycle, CMake integration, drawing, camera/input and diagnostic facilities. Workers start with [bitloop-development](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/SKILL.md), then load only references relevant to their task. Planning/review receives this catalogue, not the linked manuals.\n\nPrerequisites: follow [Substrate instructions](../../AGENTS.md), identify the active target/build and inspect its Bitloop headers for exact APIs. Hints describe framework facilities, not implemented Substrate features or successful checks. Keep simulation ownership in this project; changes to framework/other owners require the applicable authorization.\n\nEvidence: compile the smallest relevant target for wiring; use semantic checks for physics, snapshots for visual claims and measured benchmarks for performance. Record capability IDs, actual commands/configuration, observations, limits and retained evidence paths in the MindGraph task result's supported fields."
                    },
                    {
                        "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/input.md",
                        "id": "input",
                        "summary": "# Scripted input and interaction checks\n\nActions: Bitloop's development headless runner accepts `--input FILE` with frame-scheduled client-space JSONL events/gestures: mouse movement/buttons/wheel, keyboard transitions, touch and drag gestures. A task can replay dragging an object from A to B and inspect before/after snapshots plus semantic position/ownership checks.\n\nPrerequisites: a compatible development build, the correct project/scene/viewport and client dimensions, implemented interaction handlers, deterministic setup and verified replay syntax in the active framework. Follow [input/events](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/input-events.md) and [camera/viewport](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/camera-viewport.md). Coordinates are client space, not world space. Event ownership, camera navigation and UI consumption affect the result.\n\nEvidence: retain the input JSONL, launch configuration, relevant frames, resulting state observations and inspected images. A replay file or an image path alone does not prove the interaction succeeded. Substrate interaction support must be checked per task."
                    },
                    {
                        "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/simulation-health.md",
                        "id": "simulation-health",
                        "summary": "# Behavioural horizons and temporary probes\n\nActions: combine focused numerical gates, cheap per-tick invariant monitors and adaptive complete-state inspections to investigate delayed, cumulative or rare failures. Route trajectory-changing work through [simulation health](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/simulation-health-diagnostics.md); use [temporary diagnostics](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/temporary-diagnostics.md) for short-lived probes.\n\nPrerequisites: project-defined physical invariants, meaningful scales, controlled configuration/seeds and a justified simulated horizon. Monitor logic and physical oracles must be implemented for Substrate; these workflows do not imply they already exist. Put disposable probes behind `BITLOOP_DEV_MODE`, outside production hot paths, and remove them when their purpose ends.\n\nEvidence: record dt/ticks/simulated time, timescales/event opportunities, monitor coverage, inspection schedule, worst-case observations, stopping rationale and remaining uncertainty. A short smoke run or clean final screenshot is insufficient for long-horizon claims. Preserve compact findings under `.codex/runs`; keep high-volume scratch in a manifest-owned session under the Codex build directory."
                    },
                    {
                        "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/snapshots-checkpoints.md",
                        "id": "snapshots-checkpoints",
                        "summary": "# Visual snapshots and explicit checkpoints\n\nActions: capture periodic layout images or targeted scene/viewport, pixel/world-region snapshots through Bitloop's capture pipeline. Save explicit simulation checkpoints and restore selected state for reruns when the project implements serialization. Follow [snapshots/checkpoints](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/checkpoints-snapshots.md).\n\nPrerequisites: a development build, rendering support and stable unambiguous scene/viewport selection for images; implemented save/load hooks and compatible configuration/scene IDs for checkpoints. Substrate's generated checkpoint hooks initially return false. Checkpoints are not automatic memory serialization and do not prove restart equivalence.\n\nEvidence: inspect actual images and correlate them with semantic traces. For restored runs, compare uninterrupted versus save/restore continuation using invariants, state fingerprints or justified tolerances. Record configuration, frame/time, image/checkpoint paths and observed outcomes. Use [visual QA](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/visual-qa.md) when appearance is part of acceptance; pixels alone cannot establish physics correctness."
                    },
                    {
                        "hint_file": ".mindgraph/project_capabilities/traces.md",
                        "id": "traces",
                        "summary": "# Headless semantic traces\n\nActions: run Bitloop development builds headlessly, list/select stable scene IDs, supply configuration overrides, advance a fixed timestep and record bounded semantic JSONL traces. Choose sparse or focused frame capture; reconstruct compact delta traces before analysing them. Reuse [tracing diagnostics](../../.agents/skills/bitloop-development/references/tracing-diagnostics.md).\n\nPrerequisites: `BITLOOP_DEV_MODE` tooling, a runnable target, supported scene configuration and implemented scene telemetry. Substrate's generated `traceFrame` hook initially has no semantic metrics; verify/implement the metrics required by the task. The framework does not supply this engine's physical oracle automatically.\n\nEvidence: retain command/configuration, seed, scene IDs, fixed dt, simulated horizon, trace paths and observed invariants/metrics under `.codex/runs/runNNNN_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS/`. Report numerical conclusions and their tolerances, not just successful exit or trace-file existence. Use focused tests for kernels and traces for temporal behaviour."
                    }
                ],
                "snapshot_id": "fnv1a64:76d941bda9a71bde",
